    <node id="Basal Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common type of skin cancer, also known as basal cell carcinoma (BCC), which is highly treatable and curable, especially when caught early.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of skin cancer that can recur at the original site or metastasize to lymph nodes or distant parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-001</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin">
      <data key="d0">Skin</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The body's largest organ, acting as a barrier, with three layers: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis.&lt;SEP&gt;The largest organ of the body, protecting against invaders, controlling body temperature, and allowing sensations. It has three layers: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue not affected by leukemia, used to test for predisposition syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;A site where leukemia can spread outside the bloodstream.&lt;SEP&gt;The outer covering of the body, a site for HPV infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Epidermis">
      <data key="d0">Epidermis</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The top layer of skin, providing a waterproof barrier and containing squamous cells, basal cells, and melanocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;The outermost layer of skin that provides a waterproof barrier and creates skin color (melanin).&lt;SEP&gt;The top layer of skin removed during a superficial shave biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dermis">
      <data key="d0">Dermis</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The middle layer of skin, containing connective tissue, blood vessels, oil and sweat glands, nerves, and hair follicles.&lt;SEP&gt;The middle layer of skin containing connective tissue, hair follicles, and sweat glands.&lt;SEP&gt;The layer of skin beneath the epidermis, partially removed during a superficial shave biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hypodermis">
      <data key="d0">Hypodermis</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The deepest tissue layer of skin, made up of fat and connective tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;The deepest layer of skin, made up of fat and connective tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cells">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Cells found in the lower part of the epidermis that constantly divide to form new cells replacing squamous cells on the skin's surface.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Flat cells found in the upper part of the epidermis.&lt;SEP&gt;Flat, thin cells that normally make up the epithelium of the esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;Thin, flat cells lining the esophagus; origin of squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Flat cells that make up the epithelial layer; source of squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Thin, flat cells that form the surface lining of the nasopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that line the ectocervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that form the surface of the skin and other tissues; the origin of squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melanocytes">
      <data key="d0">Melanocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Cells located in the lower part of the epidermis that produce melanin, responsible for hair, skin, and eye color and protection from UV rays.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells in the top layer of skin that produce melanin, responsible for skin, hair, and eye color and protection from UV rays.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melanin">
      <data key="d0">Melanin</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A pigment responsible for hair, skin, and eye color, and filters out harmful UV rays.&lt;SEP&gt;A pigment produced in melanocytes that gives color to hair, skin, and eyes and protects skin from harmful ultraviolet rays.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="UV Radiation">
      <data key="d0">UV Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultraviolet radiation from the sun or tanning beds that can damage DNA in skin cells, leading to skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DNA">
      <data key="d0">DNA</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) is the molecule that carries genetic information in cells, serving as the fundamental blueprint for how cells grow, develop, and function. DNA is tightly packaged into chromosomes within the cell, and this genetic material is responsible for heredity and the regulation of cellular processes. Damage to DNA can cause cells to become abnormal, potentially leading to disease. Additionally, mutations in DNA can be identified through biomarker testing, and various diagnostic tests analyze DNA to detect such genetic alterations.

DNA is also the substrate for key molecular biology techniques: it is the genetic material that is sequenced by next-generation sequencing and amplified by polymerase chain reaction (PCR). As the carrier of genetic information, DNA is central to understanding biology, genetics, and disease mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682762</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery is a broad medical term for procedures that involve physical intervention, typically through an incision, to treat disease, remove cancer or other tissue, or repair the body. In oncology, it is a fundamental treatment modality used to physically remove tumors, cancerous tissue, and sometimes nearby lymph nodes. As a treatment for various cancers, its application and feasibility depend on the cancer type, stage, and location. For instance, it is the most common treatment for basal cell skin cancer and a primary treatment for early-stage cervical cancer, often involving the removal of cancerous tissue. For ovarian cancer, surgery is frequently the initial treatment, often performed by a gynecologic oncologist, and is typically followed by chemotherapy and maintenance therapy; its success depends on tumor size and location. In prostate cancer, surgery involves removing the cancerous tissue, which may include the tumor or the entire prostate, and is an option even for advanced stages. For bladder cancer, the procedure can range from removing the tumor to removing the entire bladder, depending on the stage, and may also involve removing surrounding tissue. In breast cancer, surgery is used as an initial treatment, after which pathologic staging provides more accurate details, and may involve the removal of breast tissue for analysis.

Surgery is also a key treatment for several other malignancies. It is a treatment option for early or locally advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and for some people with stage 1, 2, or 3 pancreatic cancer to remove cancer from the body. For thyroid cancer, it is a standard treatment involving the physical removal of tissue, with targeted therapy used when surgery cannot be performed. It is a main treatment for glioma, involving tumor removal, and can be the sole treatment for grade 1 gliomas. For chondrosarcoma and other bone cancers, surgery is used to remove tumors. In anal cancer, surgery may be used to remove the anus if the disease does not respond to treatment or returns. However, surgery is not always possible or successful, such as for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), and it is not normally an option for primary CNS lymphoma due to associated risks. Additionally, surgery may have increased side effects for smokers, and staging after the procedure provides more accurate details about the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-168&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682766</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation therapy is a medical treatment that uses high-energy radiation, such as x-rays, gamma rays, electrons, photons, or protons, to kill cancer cells and shrink tumors. It is a localized treatment, meaning it affects a specific area of the body. The therapy is typically administered in daily sessions over several weeks, with each session lasting between 30 and 60 minutes, and is often used alone or in combination with other treatments like surgery, chemotherapy, or systemic therapy.

This treatment is applied to a wide range of cancers, including basal cell skin cancer, cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), prostate cancer (both early and advanced stages), primary CNS lymphoma (including in the eyes, via whole-brain radiation therapy or focused eye treatment), chordoma, glioma, and anal cancer. For some cancers, such as anal cancer, it is a primary initial treatment, often combined with chemotherapy (chemoradiation). In other cases, it is recommended when surgery is not an option, as an adjuvant therapy after surgery, or as a main treatment for unresectable tumors.

While effective, radiation therapy carries potential side effects. Short-term effects can include skin rash, problems swallowing, dry mouth, thick saliva, changes in taste, and tiredness. Long-term or late-onset side effects may include vision problems, cataracts, damage to the perineum, and effects on endocrine glands and hearing when treating the head and neck. It can also cause infertility. Furthermore, prior exposure to radiation therapy is a known risk factor for developing small cell lung cancer (SCLC). The specific delivery methods include external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) and brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-109&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-193&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-195</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682768</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic Therapy is a broad category of cancer treatment that uses drugs or other substances to reach and affect cancer cells throughout the body, rather than targeting a single localized area. These therapies are typically administered intravenously or orally, allowing the medicine to travel through the bloodstream to reach cells in any part of the body. This approach is used to treat various cancers, including basal cell skin cancer, acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), Ewing sarcoma, metastatic cervical cancer, and bladder cancer, among others. Systemic therapy can be employed as a primary treatment, as an initial therapy, or before or after surgery (neoadjuvant or adjuvant settings), and may also be used as maintenance therapy to prevent recurrence.

The main types of systemic therapy include chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy. Chemotherapy uses drugs to kill rapidly dividing cancer cells, while targeted therapy focuses on specific molecules involved in cancer growth, such as HER2-targeted agents or inhibitors. Immunotherapy harnesses the body’s immune system to fight cancer. Other forms include endocrine (hormonal) therapy, which is often used in hormone-sensitive cancers, and platinum-based chemotherapy regimens. These treatments can be used alone or in combination, depending on the cancer type and stage.

Because systemic therapy affects cells throughout the body, it can impact both cancer cells and normal cells, leading to a range of potential side effects. Common short-term side effects include tiredness, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, hair loss, mouth sores, loss of appetite, and low blood cell counts. Some side effects may be delayed and occur years after treatment. Additionally, certain systemic therapies, particularly some targeted treatments, can affect fertility. The specific side effects and their severity vary based on the drugs used, the dosage, and the individual patient.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-109&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-110&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-149&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189</data>
