    <node id="Lynch Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch Syndrome is a hereditary condition caused by mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes, which leads to a deficiency in DNA repair mechanisms. This inherited syndrome is the most common cause of hereditary colon and uterine (endometrial) cancers, and it significantly increases the risk of developing colorectal, endometrial, and ovarian cancers. Beyond these primary sites, Lynch Syndrome is also associated with an elevated risk for other malignancies, including pancreatic cancer, adrenal tumors, and cancers of the urinary tract, such as bladder cancer. The condition is recognized as a key factor in clinical decision-making, particularly when considering germline testing for individuals with a personal or family history suggestive of these cancers. As an inherited cancer syndrome, Lynch Syndrome is fundamentally linked to an increased susceptibility to a broad spectrum of cancers, with colon and uterine cancers being the most prominent, followed by ovarian and other extracolonic malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682801</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
