    <node id="MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI Scan, also known as magnetic resonance imaging, is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses radio waves and powerful magnets to produce detailed pictures of the inside of the body. Unlike X-rays or CT scans, it does not use ionizing radiation, making it a safe option for many diagnostic purposes. The technology works by aligning hydrogen atoms in the body with the magnetic field and then using radiofrequency pulses to generate signals that are converted into high-resolution images by a computer. These images can be taken from various angles and provide excellent contrast between different soft tissues, making MRI particularly valuable for examining organs, muscles, and the brain.

MRI scans are widely used in medical practice for a range of applications. They are especially effective for imaging the central nervous system, where they help detect tumors, structural abnormalities, and other pathologies. In urology, MRI is used to evaluate the prostate and surrounding areas, providing detailed views that assist in diagnosing conditions and guiding procedures. For example, MRI can be combined with ultrasound to improve the accuracy of prostate biopsies, and it is also used to measure prostate size, which is essential for calculating PSA density—a key metric in prostate cancer assessment.

In clinical practice, MRI scans often involve the use of contrast agents to enhance image clarity and highlight specific areas of interest. There are two main types of MRI machines: closed and open designs. Closed MRI systems, which feature a narrow, tunnel-like bore, are more common and generally produce higher-quality images, though they may cause discomfort for claustrophobic patients. Open MRI machines offer a more spacious environment but may have lower image resolution. Overall, MRI is a versatile and radiation-free imaging modality that plays a critical role in modern diagnostics across multiple medical specialties.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-086&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-102&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155</data>
