      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET Scan, or Positron Emission Tomography scan, is a nuclear medicine imaging test that uses a radioactive drug, known as a tracer or radiotracer, to observe metabolic processes and cellular activity within the body. The primary clinical application of a PET scan is in oncology, where it is used to visualize, locate, and assess cancerous cells. The procedure typically involves injecting a tracer, often a form of radioactive sugar, which is taken up by cells. Because malignant cells generally consume glucose at a higher rate than normal tissue, they accumulate more of the tracer and consequently appear as "bright spots" on the resulting images. This brightness allows physicians to identify where cancer cells are located and gauge how fast they are growing based on the amount of sugar they absorb.

While a PET scan is highly effective at highlighting areas of high metabolic activity, the appearance of bright spots is not exclusively indicative of cancer. Normal physiological activity in organs such as the brain, heart, kidneys, and bladder can also produce bright signals, as can areas of inflammation or infection. Therefore, while the scan is a powerful tool for detecting potentially cancerous cells, not all bright spots are malignant. To improve diagnostic accuracy and provide anatomical context, a PET scan is frequently combined with other imaging modalities, most commonly computed tomography (CT) in a PET/CT scan, or sometimes with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). This combination allows for the precise localization of the metabolic hotspots identified by the PET scan, which is particularly valuable for cancer staging, treatment planning, and monitoring disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-102&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155</data>
