    <node id="Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer is a broad class of diseases characterized by uncontrolled or abnormal cell growth, where cells divide out of control due to genetic changes, and can invade nearby tissues, spread, and harm the body. This condition can affect various parts of the body, may run in families, and encompasses many specific types, including chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), primary CNS lymphoma, and breast cancer, among others. Cancer presents in various stages, including metastatic and numbered stages, and can cause significant physical effects, such as bone damage, as well as emotional and psychological distress for patients. The disease can also impact fertility, often requiring medical treatment that addresses both the cancer and its side effects.

Diagnosis and management of cancer rely heavily on a range of medical techniques, including various imaging methods and biopsy procedures, which are used to detect, characterize, and monitor the disease. Treatment approaches are diverse and are discussed extensively in medical contexts, aiming to control or eliminate the abnormal cell growth. Given its complexity, cancer is a central subject of medical texts, which explore its testing, treatment, and overall impact on patients' lives.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-102&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-156&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-195&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682835</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tracer">
      <data key="d0">Tracer</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Tracer is a radioactive substance or drug that is administered into the body, typically via intravenous injection, for use in Positron Emission Tomography (PET) scans. Its primary function is to help visualize and locate cancer cells within the body. The substance is designed to attach to or be taken up by cells, often leveraging a sugar-based compound, which allows the scan to measure the metabolic activity of cells, specifically their glucose uptake. This capability enables medical professionals to identify where cancer cells are present and assess how much sugar they consume, providing critical diagnostic information.

In addition to its role in oncology, Tracer can also be used to detect areas of bone damage. In this context, the substance accumulates in regions where bone injury or abnormality exists, making these areas clearly visible on the resulting scan images. Overall, Tracer serves as a crucial diagnostic tool in nuclear medicine, aiding in the detection, localization, and characterization of cancerous tissues and bone pathologies through advanced imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-156</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT scan">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of PET scan that uses radioactive sugar (18-fluorodeoxyglucose) as a tracer to detect active cancer cells, which appear brighter in images. It is used to determine if neuroendocrine tumors are becoming more aggressive.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="SSTR-PET/CT scan">
      <data key="d0">SSTR-PET/CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of PET scan that uses a tracer binding to somatostatin receptors (SSTRs) on cell surfaces, helping to locate cancer cells, particularly for adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests used to look for signs of hormone secretion or to assess response to treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests that analyze blood samples for signs of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests that provide information about general health and organ function before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biochemical tests">
      <data key="d0">Biochemical tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests that measure substances like hormones in blood, urine, and/or saliva that may be produced by adrenal tumors, helping to diagnose adrenal tumors or related syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous cell skin cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous cell skin cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The second most common type of skin cancer, highly treatable and often curable, also known as cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC).</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basal cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Basal cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common type of skin cancer, mentioned as a comparison to squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melanoma">
      <data key="d0">Melanoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A less common but potentially more aggressive type of skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of skin cancer that can show p16 expression.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of skin cancer; mentioned as related to certain germline mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of skin cancer associated with BRCA mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of skin cancer. NCCN Guidelines for Patients are available on this cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nonmelanoma skin cancers">
      <data key="d0">Nonmelanoma skin cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of skin cancers that includes basal cell and squamous cell skin cancers, also called keratinocyte carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Keratinocyte carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Keratinocyte carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Another term for nonmelanoma skin cancers, including basal cell and squamous cell skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Another name for squamous cell skin cancer, used interchangeably in the guide.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous cells">
      <data key="d0">Squamous cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Thin, flat cells that look like fish scales, found in the tissue forming the surface of the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basal cells">
      <data key="d0">Basal cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Cells found in the lower part of the epidermis that constantly divide to form new cells, eventually becoming squamous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sunlight/outdoor tanning">
      <data key="d0">Sunlight/outdoor tanning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A major risk factor for squamous cell skin cancer, caused by prolonged sun exposure, especially in fair-skinned people.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Indoor tanning">
      <data key="d0">Indoor tanning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A major risk factor for squamous cell skin cancer, involving the use of tanning machines like booths and beds.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental toxins">
      <data key="d0">Environmental toxins</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pollutants such as tobacco smoke, vehicle exhaust, and arsenic that can cause skin damage leading to squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scars and chronic wounds">
      <data key="d0">Scars and chronic wounds</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Conditions such as ulcers and burns that can lead to the formation of CSCC, known as Marjolin ulcer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of skin cancer that can form in scars or chronic wounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Somatostatin receptor (SSTR)">
      <data key="d0">Somatostatin receptor (SSTR)</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A protein on the surface of cells that binds to the hormone somatostatin, found on many cell types.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Somatostatin">
      <data key="d0">Somatostatin</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A hormone that helps control other hormones in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="18-fluorodeoxyglucose">
      <data key="d0">18-fluorodeoxyglucose</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A radioactive type of sugar used as a tracer in FDG-PET scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal tumors">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors that develop in the adrenal glands, which may secrete hormones and can be detected using various imaging and biochemical tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine tumors">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors that arise from cells of the neuroendocrine system, which can be assessed for aggressiveness using FDG-PET scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Guide 1 Biochemical tests">
      <data key="d0">Guide 1 Biochemical tests</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A guide that provides more information on biochemical tests for adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that starts in squamous cells, which can occur in the skin (cutaneous) or other areas like lungs, thyroid, and esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma that arises in the skin, also known as CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basal cell layer">
      <data key="d0">Basal cell layer</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The lower part of the epidermis where basal cells are located.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ultraviolet (UV) rays">
      <data key="d0">Ultraviolet (UV) rays</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Harmful rays from the sun that can damage skin and are protected against by melanin.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco smoke">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A pollutant that can cause skin damage leading to squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arsenic">
      <data key="d0">Arsenic</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemical element found in soil, water, and air that is an environmental toxin linked to skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element found in soil, water, and air that is an environmental toxin and can lead to skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A cancer-causing agent that increases lung cancer risk, especially in smokers.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A general term for cancer that forms in the tissues of the skin. The text discusses risk factors and types, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The most common cancer in American males, mentioned as a comparison to prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that develops from skin cells, distinct from primary cutaneous lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682837</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kidneys">
      <data key="d0">Kidneys</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidneys are a pair of organs located in the abdomen that play a central role in the urinary system. Their primary function is to filter blood, removing waste products and excess water to produce urine, which then drains through the ureters and, in some cases, a urinary diversion. Beyond filtration, the kidneys also produce hormones and are part of the broader urothelial lining. In medical imaging, the kidneys normally appear bright on PET scans and are involved in the excretion of contrast materials. Their function is a critical consideration in the use of contrast agents, as poor or impaired kidney function may preclude the administration of contrast material. Kidney function is routinely assessed through blood tests, including a chemistry profile or a comprehensive metabolic panel. Additionally, the kidneys are vulnerable to damage from certain treatments, such as high-dose methotrexate, which can potentially lead to kidney failure. The kidneys are examined through various procedures, including ureteroscopy and imaging techniques, to evaluate their structure and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-102&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Toxins">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Toxins</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pollutants such as tobacco smoke, vehicle exhaust, and arsenic that can cause skin damage leading to squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scars">
      <data key="d0">Scars</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Areas of fibrous tissue that replace normal skin after injury. Chronic scars can be a site for squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A common side effect of any surgery, including ovarian cancer surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Wounds">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Wounds</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nonhealing wounds such as ulcers and burns that can develop into squamous cell carcinoma, known as Marjolin's ulcer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin's Ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin's Ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of squamous cell carcinoma that arises in chronic wounds, ulcers, or scars.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer that forms in old wounds, burns, or scars, harder to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of squamous cell skin cancer that forms in old wounds, burns, or scars, and can be harder to treat.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-007</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratosis">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rough, scaly patch of skin caused by sun exposure that may develop into squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;An area of scaly or rough skin with a higher chance of becoming squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A rough, scaly patch on the skin caused by years of sun exposure, considered precancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-008</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited conditions that increase the risk of developing squamous cell skin cancer, such as Xeroderma Pigmentosum and Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa.&lt;SEP&gt;Certain inherited conditions that increase risk of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Xeroderma Pigmentosum">
      <data key="d0">Xeroderma Pigmentosum</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic condition where the body cannot repair DNA damage caused by sunlight, leading to skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic condition that increases sensitivity to radiation and skin cancer risk, making radiation therapy inappropriate.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-009</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa">
      <data key="d0">Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare inherited skin disorder causing fragile skin and blisters, increasing risk of squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Weakened Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Weakened Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A state where the body's immune system is compromised, increasing susceptibility to infections and cancers like squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition that increases the risk of getting CSCC and of cancer returning after treatment, with examples like corticosteroid use, HIV/AIDS, or organ transplant.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immunosuppressants">
      <data key="d0">Immunosuppressants</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Drugs taken after organ transplantation to prevent rejection, which also reduce the body's ability to fight infections and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Anti-rejection medicines taken after an organ transplant that weaken the immune system and increase the risk of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplantation">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplantation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical procedure where an organ is transferred from one person to another, requiring immunosuppressant drugs.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer of the lymph nodes that can weaken the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that begins when white blood cells called lymphocytes grow out of control. Lymphomas are described as either Hodgkin or non-Hodgkin.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer of lymphocytes that can develop when lymphocytes grow without regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that forms from lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;An abnormal overgrowth (cancer) of lymphocytes, which are white blood cells that fight infections.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that can occur in the central nervous system, often presenting as one or multiple tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of blood cancer that is a risk factor for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer of white blood cells in the bone marrow that can weaken the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, characterized by changes in bone marrow and abnormal B cells. It is often referred to as CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that affects lymphocytes; often abbreviated as CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of blood cancer that is a risk factor for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HIV/AIDS">
      <data key="d0">HIV/AIDS</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome, which weaken the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunodeficiency condition that increases cervical cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure to remove a sample of skin lesion for examination under a microscope to diagnose skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove a sample of skin for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove a sample of skin and test it for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove a small sample of tissue for testing to diagnose squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy that takes a small sample of inflamed skin on the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to sample inflamed skin on the breast to diagnose inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer, also referred to as CSCC, is a type of cancer that arises from squamous cells in the skin. It is the second most common form of skin cancer and is highly treatable, particularly when detected early. The condition is most often caused by cumulative sun exposure, and it can also develop in areas of skin that have been damaged by ultraviolet radiation. In addition to sun-damaged skin, CSCC can form in scars, chronic wounds, old burns, or other areas of prior skin injury; cases arising in such contexts may be more challenging to treat.

The disease has the potential to spread, or metastasize, to lymph nodes and other parts of the body, which underscores the importance of timely intervention. Treatment options for squamous cell skin cancer include topical therapies and surgical removal, with the choice of approach depending on the lesion's characteristics and location. The condition encompasses various subtypes, and risk factors—such as history of skin damage or chronic inflammation—are important considerations in its development and management. Overall, while CSCC is a serious malignancy, its prognosis is generally favorable with appropriate and prompt care.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682842</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Shave Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Shave Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of skin biopsy that removes the top layer of skin using a tool similar to a razor.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682842</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Punch Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Punch Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of skin biopsy that removes a small piece of skin and connective tissue using a hand-held tool, often requiring stitches.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure where a small piece of skin and connective tissue are removed using a hand-held tool, often closed with stitches.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove a small piece of skin and connective tissue to obtain DNA not altered by AML, used to test for inherited predisposition.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove a small skin sample for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove a small piece of skin and connective tissue to obtain DNA that hasn't been altered by AML, used to test for inherited genetic changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-071</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682842</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Excision Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excision Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of skin biopsy that removes all or part of the lesion.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure that removes all or part of a lesion, can be done in a doctor's office or hospital.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682842</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologist">
      <data key="d0">Pathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathologist is a medical professional and physician expert who specializes in diagnosing diseases, particularly cancer, by examining cells and tissues. They analyze biopsy samples, tissue samples, and other specimens—such as lymph node, bone marrow, and urine samples—under a microscope to detect abnormal cells, confirm the presence of cancer, and determine tumor type, subtype, histology, and grade. This involves comparing cancer cells to normal cells to assess cancer characteristics and assign specific grading systems, such as Gleason patterns for prostate cancer. Pathologists also examine removed tissue to determine cancer stage and test lymph nodes for cancer spread. As part of a multidisciplinary care team, they analyze cells, tissues, and organs removed during biopsies or surgeries, providing essential diagnostic information, staging, and biomarker testing results. They compile their findings into a pathology report, which guides treatment decisions. Their role is critical in confirming cancer diagnoses, identifying tumor subtypes, and evaluating disease progression across various cancer types, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-086&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682844</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical facility specialized in cancer prevention and treatment, recommended by NCCN for people with genetic syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;A facility where PET/CT imaging may be performed with one or two machines.&lt;SEP&gt;A facility specializing in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682844</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN">
      <data key="d0">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an alliance of cancer centers that develops and publishes expert recommendations and guidelines for cancer care. Its primary role is to provide evidence-based treatment guidelines, which are widely used by healthcare professionals to determine appropriate therapies, including chemotherapy regimens. NCCN also offers patient-specific guidelines and resources, making complex medical information accessible to individuals affected by cancer.

In addition to its clinical guidelines, NCCN provides patient education materials, including an app designed to deliver cancer information and assist with managing treatment side effects. The organization also facilitates patient feedback through surveys, which can be accessed via its website at NCCN.org/patients/feedback. These resources aim to support patients in understanding their diagnosis, treatment options, and care pathways, including referrals to specialized cancer centers and genetic counseling for individuals with inherited cancer syndromes.

Overall, NCCN functions as a comprehensive resource hub, bridging expert medical knowledge and patient support. Its dual focus on professional treatment standards and patient empowerment underscores its commitment to improving cancer care quality and patient experience across the continuum of care.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-197</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Geneticist">
      <data key="d0">Medical Geneticist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A physician specialized in genetics who may provide counseling for those with genetic risk of skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical specialist in hormones and metabolism who may be involved in genetic counseling for skin cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical specialist on the thyroid cancer treatment team who manages hormone-related aspects.&lt;SEP&gt;A doctor who specializes in hormone-related conditions, including thyroid disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682847</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Counselor is a healthcare professional with specialized training in genetic diseases and cancer genetics. This expert provides counseling and guidance to individuals and families regarding genetic risks, particularly those associated with inherited syndromes and hereditary cancers, such as skin cancer and rectal cancer. The Genetic Counselor assesses inherited cancer risks, advises on the appropriateness of genetic testing, and helps patients decide whether to undergo procedures like germline testing. They are also skilled in interpreting genetic test results and explaining how changes in genes relate to disease, ensuring patients understand the implications for their health. Additionally, this professional interprets how family history may impact treatment options and provides comprehensive support for managing genetic predispositions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682849</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical doctor who performs operations and may be involved in genetic counseling for skin cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical professional who performs surgical procedures such as sentinel lymph node biopsy and wound closure.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical professional who performs surgical procedures, involved in diagnosing IBC.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical professional who performs surgery to remove sentinel lymph nodes and tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical specialist on the thyroid cancer treatment team who performs surgical procedures like thyroidectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A doctor who performs surgical operations.&lt;SEP&gt;A doctor who performs surgical biopsies and may perform core needle biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-156</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682849</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nurse">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nurse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nurse specialized in cancer care who may provide counseling for genetic risk of skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A nurse who provides hands-on care, such as giving systemic therapy, managing care, answering questions, and helping with side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682849</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local Anesthetic">
      <data key="d0">Local Anesthetic</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Numbing medicine injected into the skin before a biopsy to prevent pain.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682849</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncologist is a physician who specializes in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer. This medical professional is involved in multiple stages of cancer care, including initial diagnosis, treatment planning, and ongoing management. The oncologist receives and interprets imaging results, often provided by a radiologist, to assess the disease and guide therapeutic decisions. In addition to imaging, the oncologist collaborates with a hematopathologist to examine blood samples, ensuring accurate diagnosis and monitoring of the condition.

Beyond direct treatment, the oncologist performs physical exams and follow-up visits during the surveillance phase of care, monitoring patients for recurrence or complications. They may also provide counseling regarding genetic risks, such as susceptibility to skin cancer, and offer guidance on preventive measures. As part of comprehensive care, the oncologist develops a survivorship care plan and coordinates with the primary care doctor to ensure long-term health management after active treatment concludes. Overall, the oncologist serves as a central figure in the cancer care team, integrating specialized knowledge with collaborative practice to support patients from diagnosis through survivorship.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Dye">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Dye</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A liquid injected into a vein to help tissues and organs show up more clearly in CT and MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A very common type of skin cancer, occurring more often in lighter-skinned people, commonly on sun-exposed areas, rarely spreads but can be severe if untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of skin cancer (CSCC) that can be treated with surgery, radiation therapy, and systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-009</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MRI">
      <data key="d0">MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a non-invasive medical imaging technique that uses a powerful magnet, radio waves, and a computer to create detailed, cross-sectional pictures of areas inside the body, including organs, soft tissues, bones, and internal structures. Unlike X-rays or CT scans, MRI does not use ionizing radiation, making it a safe option for repeated imaging. The technique encompasses both closed and open types of machines and can be performed with or without contrast material, which is often administered to enhance the visibility of specific tissues or abnormalities. MRI is widely used to detect, diagnose, and stage various conditions, including cancers, and is particularly valuable for examining soft tissue contrast.

In oncology, MRI plays a critical role in cancer detection, staging, and treatment planning. It is used to assess the depth of tumor invasion and lymph node involvement, notably in rectal cancer staging, and to detect cancer spread in the spine and brain. MRI is considered the most useful imaging test for identifying primary CNS lymphoma and gliomas, as it provides high-resolution images of the brain and spinal cord. For breast tissue examination, MRI helps detect abnormalities and can guide biopsies. Additionally, MRI is employed to guide applicator placement and design brachytherapy treatment plans, and it can be combined with computerized navigation systems to precisely guide biopsies. It is also used for further imaging of suspicious lymph nodes and for imaging surveillance, often with contrast, to monitor disease progression or recurrence. In some cases, MRI is combined with PET or CT to provide complementary anatomical and functional information, enhancing diagnostic accuracy across a range of clinical applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-113&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-114&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-166&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682854</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure using a needle to take a sample from lymph nodes for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove tissue or fluid from the breast for examination.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove a small sample of tissue using a needle for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy using a needle to take a small sample from a lymph node.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682854</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Local Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682854</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682854</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nodal Disease">
      <data key="d0">Nodal Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Condition where cancer has spread to lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer present in lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682854</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph">
      <data key="d0">Lymph</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph is a clear, watery fluid that circulates throughout the body via the lymphatic system, a network of vessels and nodes. It is also known as lymphatic fluid. Its primary functions are to provide cells with water and essential nutrients (food), and to help the body fight infections and germs. This is achieved by transporting infection-fighting white blood cells, which are a key component of the fluid. Lymph is drained and carried through lymphatic vessels and passes through lymph nodes, which help filter the fluid and support immune responses. 

In the context of disease, lymph is the medium through which cancer cells can travel and spread from their original site to other parts of the body, a process known as metastasis. While most descriptions consistently identify lymph as a clear fluid that nourishes cells and carries immune cells, there is a notable discrepancy: one source incorrectly states that lymph is produced by the liver, whereas the lymphatic system is the correct origin. Additionally, some descriptions specify that lymph helps fight germs or tumor cells, while others focus solely on its role in fighting infection. Overall, lymph is essential for both immune defense and the transport of substances, including potentially harmful cancer cells, throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682857</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Doctor is a medical professional who provides comprehensive patient care, including performing diagnostic procedures such as biopsies, digital rectal exams, and laryngoscopy, as well as interpreting their results. This physician advises on treatment options, including birth control choices, and considers risk factors for conditions like cervical cancer. The doctor orders imaging tests, discusses fertility risks, and informs patients about treatment plans, while also receiving and reviewing reports from radiologists to oversee patient care and discuss findings with the patient. Additionally, the doctor recommends diagnostic tests for conditions such as bladder cancer, may suggest genetic testing, and provides guidance on lifestyle factors like exercise, diet, aspirin use, and smoking cessation, alongside cancer treatment decisions. For specialized cases, this medical professional should have experience in diagnosing and treating specific types of bone cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-082&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682859</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Patient">
      <data key="d0">Patient</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient is an individual receiving medical care, typically within the context of a suspected or confirmed cancer diagnosis, including specific types such as cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), mouth cancer, kidney cancer, breast cancer, and bone cancer. This person may be undergoing evaluation, diagnostic testing, medical imaging, and various medical procedures, and is actively engaged with a care team throughout the process. The patient may also be involved in breast cancer screening and other preventive or diagnostic measures.

In addition to receiving care, the patient is an active participant in their healthcare journey, often encouraged to advocate for themselves, ask questions, and engage in treatment planning. As someone diagnosed with cancer, the patient receives medical advice and is involved in making informed treatment decisions. Overall, the patient is the central individual undergoing tests, interacting with healthcare providers, and receiving comprehensive care tailored to their specific condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682861</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Cells are abnormal cells characterized by uncontrolled growth and division, often resulting from genetic mutations or errors. Unlike normal cells, they break the standard rules of cell growth, multiply rapidly, and can avoid the natural process of cell death. These cells can crowd out and damage normal cells, leading to the formation of tumors and causing various symptoms. A defining feature of cancer cells is their ability to spread, or metastasize, to other parts of the body through the bloodstream or the lymphatic system. Lymph nodes can filter out these cells as they travel, which is a key part of how cancer spreads and is detected.

Cancer cells exhibit distinct behaviors that aid in their detection and treatment. They consume sugar at a higher rate than normal cells, causing them to appear as bright spots on PET scans, a common imaging test used for detection. Their rapid growth and spread make them targets for aggressive treatments, such as high-dose chemotherapy, which is designed to kill these dividing cells. The presence and behavior of cancer cells are central to diagnostic procedures, including imaging tests, and to the development of therapeutic strategies aimed at controlling or eliminating them.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682864</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The immune system is the body’s comprehensive defense system against infection and disease, functioning as a natural and adaptive mechanism to protect overall health. It comprises multiple integrated components, including lymph nodes, lymphocytes, and blood, all of which play essential roles in detecting and neutralizing pathogens. The lymphatic system is a major part of this defense network, contributing to immune surveillance and response. As the body’s primary defense mechanism, the immune system is capable of clearing infections such as human papillomavirus (HPV) in most individuals, demonstrating its effectiveness in pathogen elimination.

Beyond its baseline functions, the immune system is central to various medical interventions. For instance, it can be suppressed by PD-L1 expression on cancer cells, a mechanism that tumors exploit to evade immune attack. Conversely, the immune system can be deliberately triggered or enhanced for therapeutic purposes: immunotherapy leverages its natural abilities to fight cancer, while Bacillus Calmette–Guérin (BCG) treatment can activate the immune system to attack cancer cells. These examples highlight the immune system’s dual role as both a target for disease suppression and a powerful tool for treatment, underscoring its dynamic and clinically significant nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105</data>
