    </node>
    <node id="AIDS">
      <data key="d0">AIDS</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome, a condition that weakens the immune system and increases risk of CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome caused by HIV.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682894</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Connective Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Connective Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Connective Tissue is a fundamental biological material that serves to support, connect, and surround other tissues and structures within the body. It is found in a wide variety of anatomical locations, including the layers of the gallbladder, bile ducts, stomach wall, and bladder wall. In the context of the bladder, it is the specific layer of tissue that stage 1 cancer invades. Within the breast, connective tissue acts as a supporting framework that holds structures together, surrounds other components, and includes ligaments. Additionally, it is the tissue obtained from a skin punch biopsy, which is used to extract DNA for analysis. In pathology, connective tissue can also be fibrous tissue that some tumors form, and its presence in a tumor is an indicator of a high-risk or very high-risk cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682895</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Electrodesiccation">
      <data key="d0">Electrodesiccation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure that uses an electric current to destroy cancer cells, often used with curettage.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682895</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical procedure that can weaken the immune system, increasing the risk of CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical procedure that requires immunosuppressive medications, increasing infection risk.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical procedure that may require immunosuppressants, increasing the risk of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682895</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Corticosteroid Use">
      <data key="d0">Corticosteroid Use</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-term use of corticosteroids can weaken the immune system, increasing the risk of CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682895</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nicotine">
      <data key="d0">Nicotine</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicotine is a chemical compound found in tobacco that is recognized as the primary agent responsible for the addictive nature of tobacco use. It is present in both combustible tobacco products, such as cigarettes, and in vaping products. The addictive properties of nicotine make quitting smoking or vaping particularly difficult for users, as it creates a physical and psychological dependence. This challenge is further compounded by the withdrawal symptoms associated with nicotine cessation, which can include irritability, anxiety, and intense cravings. The difficulty of overcoming nicotine addiction is especially pronounced for certain populations, such as cancer patients, for whom quitting can be an exceptionally demanding process. Overall, nicotine's role as an addictive substance is the central factor that complicates efforts to stop using tobacco and vaping products.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682898</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking">
      <data key="d0">Smoking</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking is the act of inhaling and exhaling smoke from tobacco products, and it is a major behavioral risk factor for numerous cancers and other diseases. Objectively, smoking is identified as the main cause of small cell lung cancer (SCLC) and the greatest risk factor for bladder cancer, accounting for about half of all cases. It also increases the risks of developing and dying from prostate cancer, and is a significant risk factor for cervical cancer, anal cancer, and cancers of the head and neck, lung, and other sites.

Beyond increasing cancer incidence, smoking adversely affects cancer treatment. It can limit the effectiveness of therapies, increase treatment-related side effects and toxicity, and impair wound healing. Consequently, smoking is a habit that negatively impacts overall treatment outcomes. Conversely, quitting smoking can lead to better cancer treatment results and reduced health risks. As a health-related behavior, smoking is generally advised to be quit, with counseling support recommended to assist individuals in overcoming the habit.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-175&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-195&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682900</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaping is the act of inhaling vapor produced by electronic cigarettes or similar devices, a practice that is also commonly referred to as the use of e-cigarettes. From a medical standpoint, vaping is strongly discouraged, particularly for individuals diagnosed with cancer. The activity is associated with health risks that are comparable to those of traditional smoking, including a documented increase in cancer risk. Furthermore, vaping negatively impacts cancer treatment by potentially limiting the effectiveness of therapies and adversely affecting treatment outcomes. It is also known to impair the body’s natural healing processes. Consequently, medical guidance recommends that individuals cease vaping, as its continuation can interfere with recovery and compromise the success of ongoing medical care.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)">
      <data key="d0">Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure that scrapes away cancer cells with a curette and uses an electric needle to destroy remaining cancer cells, used for low-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Local CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma that is localized and has not spread to regional lymph nodes or distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of local CSCC with features that increase the risk of recurrence or spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Very High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Very High-Risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of local CSCC with the highest risk features, requiring aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Low-Risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of local CSCC with features that indicate a low risk of recurrence or spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Regional CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Regional CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography (CT) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography (CT) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An imaging test that uses X-rays to create detailed pictures of the body, used to assess lymph nodes in regional CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;An imaging technique that takes multiple x-ray pictures to create 3-D images of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An advanced imaging test that combines PET and CT to detect cancer spread, using injectable agents.&lt;SEP&gt;A combined imaging test using positron emission tomography and computed tomography to detect cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy technique that uses a thin needle to remove fluid or cells from a suspicious area, also known as needle biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy technique using a fine needle to remove a sample of tumor or lymph node.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy type using a thin needle to remove a sample of tissue or fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy technique using a thin needle to remove a sample of tumor or lymph node. It is one of the needle biopsy types mentioned.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy technique using a thin needle to extract samples from suspicious thyroid nodules, often guided by ultrasound.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure using a thin needle to remove a sample of tissue from a lymph node for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy technique that uses a larger needle to remove a core of tissue for examination.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure using a wide needle to remove a tissue sample from a lymph node without removing the entire node.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of biopsy using a core needle to obtain a tissue sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab-Rwlc (Libtayo)">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab-Rwlc (Libtayo)</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An immune checkpoint inhibitor drug used to treat certain skin cancers, sometimes given before surgery to shrink tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors located in the head and neck region, which may require lymph node removal from the neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Extracapsular Extension">
      <data key="d0">Extracapsular Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The spread of cancer through the protective outer layer of a lymph node, also called extranodal extension.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that has grown through the protective outer layer of a lymph node, also called extranodal extension.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An assessment to determine if a patient is a candidate for surgery to remove cancerous lesions and nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Doctors">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Doctors</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical professionals specializing in cancer treatment, involved in discussions for determining next steps.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical professionals who specialize in cancer treatment and may discuss treatment options for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A special radiation technique that delivers high doses to small areas, typically over 3 to 5 treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Exams">
      <data key="d0">Skin Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Regular check-ups of the skin and lymph nodes to monitor for recurrence of skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Regular examinations of the skin by a doctor to check for signs of skin cancer recurrence or new skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Experts">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Experts from the National Comprehensive Cancer Network who provide recommendations for cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;A group of experts from the National Comprehensive Cancer Network who provide recommendations for cancer care, including follow-up schedules for skin cancer and chronic myeloid leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Experts from the National Comprehensive Cancer Network who recommend considering clinical trials for primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of blood cancer characterized by an overproduction of granulocytes, caused by the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene and Philadelphia chromosome.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, characterized by the presence of the Philadelphia chromosome and BCR::ABL1 gene.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of blood cancer caused by the BCR::ABL1 gene and Philadelphia chromosome.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1 Gene">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1 Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A fused gene created by the translocation of pieces of chromosome 9 and 22, which causes chronic myeloid leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A gene that causes the BCR::ABL protein, associated with a type of ALL. Treatment aims to stop its activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A shortened chromosome 22 resulting from the translocation that creates the BCR::ABL1 gene, present in chronic myeloid leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;An abnormal chromosome 22 created by translocation, hallmark of CML.&lt;SEP&gt;A shortened chromosome 22 resulting from translocation.&lt;SEP&gt;An abnormal chromosome 22 resulting from the translocation with chromosome 9, which is the hallmark of CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682901</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-Based Chemotherapy is a category of systemic therapy that utilizes platinum-containing drugs, such as cisplatin and carboplatin, to treat various types of cancer. This approach is frequently employed as a first-line or initial treatment for several malignancies, including ovarian cancer, where it serves as a primary therapeutic option. In the context of cervical cancer, platinum-based chemotherapy is the most commonly used systemic therapy, often applied to manage metastatic forms of the disease. Additionally, it is recommended for use in combination with radiation therapy for certain cases of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), enhancing the effectiveness of treatment. Beyond these applications, this chemotherapy class is also established as a first-line therapy for metastatic anal cancer. Overall, platinum-based regimens play a critical role across multiple cancer types, either as standalone treatments or in conjunction with other modalities like radiation, underscoring their versatility and importance in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682904</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Intermediary cells derived from blood stem cells that mature into specific blood cell types, including lymphoid and myeloid progenitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediary cells that become red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets; include lymphoid and myeloid progenitor cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells derived from blood stem cells that are committed to differentiate into specific blood cell types.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediary cells that become specific blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediary cells that become red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets; includes lymphoid and myeloid progenitor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682904</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blast Phase CML">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An advanced form of chronic myeloid leukemia characterized by an increased amount of immature cells (blasts).&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced form of CML with increased lymphoid or myeloid progenitor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-016</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682904</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Stem Cells, also known as hematopoietic stem cells, are early, immature blood-forming cells located primarily in the bone marrow, including the red marrow. They serve as the foundational origin for all types of blood cells, possessing the unique ability to self-renew and differentiate through various maturation stages into red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets. These cells multiply and mature to maintain the body’s blood supply. Additionally, Blood Stem Cells are identified as the likely cellular origin of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML), as they give rise to myeloid progenitor cells, which are implicated in the disease’s development.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682905</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Blood Cells, also known as erythrocytes, are specialized blood cells whose primary function is to carry oxygen throughout the body. These cells are derived from blood stem cells and are a fundamental component of the circulatory system. Their oxygen-carrying capacity is routinely assessed through a Complete Blood Count (CBC) test, which measures their concentration in the blood. In certain medical conditions, such as Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL), red blood cell counts may be abnormally low, a condition that can be monitored through this same diagnostic measure.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682907</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">White Blood Cells, also known as leukocytes, are the infection-fighting cells of the blood and a crucial component of the immune system. They are primarily responsible for defending the body against infectious diseases and foreign germs. These cells are derived from blood stem cells and are found circulating in the bloodstream as well as within the lymphatic system, where they act as infection-fighting cells in the lymph.

White blood cells are categorized into several main types, including granulocytes, monocytes, and lymphocytes. Lymphocytes are a specific and vital subtype of white blood cell. Because of their role in immunity, the antigens present on white blood cells serve as important biomarkers. Their concentration in the blood is a key health indicator, commonly measured as part of a Complete Blood Count (CBC) test.

The behavior and counts of white blood cells are clinically significant in various diseases. For instance, Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) originates in these cells, specifically in lymphocytes. Conversely, in Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL), white blood cell counts are often found to be abnormally high.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682909</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Granulocytes">
      <data key="d0">Granulocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of white blood cell that includes neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils; often increased in CML.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of white blood cell that includes neutrophils, helping prevent and control infection.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of white blood cell that fights infections.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of white blood cell that includes neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of white blood cell, also known as neutrophils, that help prevent and control infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682909</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Progenitor cells that form into lymphoblasts and mature into lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Progenitor cells that form into lymphoblasts that mature into lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that develop into lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell. ALL arises from stem cells that produce an increased amount of these cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Progenitor cells that form into lymphoblasts that mature into lymphocytes. ALL is thought to arise in these cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Progenitor cells that form lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that can be increased in blast phase CML, leading to lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682909</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Progenitor cells that form into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that form into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that develop into red blood cells, granulocytes, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that develop into red blood cells, granulocytes, and platelets, and are affected by AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Progenitor cells that form non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that form into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells; increased in CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682909</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Very immature blood cells (myeloblasts or lymphoblasts) that are often increased in CML, especially in blast phase.&lt;SEP&gt;Immature white blood cells committed to becoming a type of blood cell.&lt;SEP&gt;Immature leukemia cells that can be detected in the blood and reported as a percentage.&lt;SEP&gt;Immature cells in the bone marrow or blood; presence of 20% or more indicates AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia cells that can sometimes be detected in the blood and usually are reported as a percentage of cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immature blood cells, either myeloblasts or lymphoblasts, often referred to simply as blasts in CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682909</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets">
      <data key="d0">Platelets</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets, also known as thrombocytes, are small, non-nucleated cell fragments derived from blood stem cells (specifically megakaryocytes) that circulate in the blood. Their primary function is to control and prevent bleeding by adhering to damaged blood vessel walls and aggregating to form blood clots, a process essential for hemostasis and wound healing. By clumping together, they create a physical plug that stops or prevents hemorrhage, thereby helping to control bleeding.

Platelets are a critical component of the blood, and their quantity is routinely measured as part of a complete blood count (CBC). Their levels can be affected by various medical conditions and treatments; for instance, targeted therapies may reduce platelet counts, increasing the risk of bruising and bleeding. Additionally, low platelet counts are commonly observed in chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL). While often referred to as blood cells, they are technically cell fragments, and their role in clotting is vital for both normal wound repair and the prevention of excessive blood loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682911</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow is the soft, sponge-like tissue located in the center of most bones, where blood cells are produced from blood-forming stem cells. It contains blood vessels and exists in two primary types: red marrow, which is active in blood cell formation, and yellow marrow, which is primarily fatty. As a key component of the lymphatic system, bone marrow is responsible for generating all types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, as well as B cells, which are crucial for immune function.

Bone marrow is clinically significant as the site of origin for several hematologic malignancies. In acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), the bone marrow produces an excess of immature lymphocytes, while in acute myeloid leukemia (AML), it accumulates abnormal blasts. It is also the tissue where chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)-associated changes occur and where tumors such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) can be found. Additionally, bone marrow is the primary site where leukemia originates, making it a critical target for diagnosis and treatment.

Diagnostically, bone marrow samples are essential for karyotype analysis, as this procedure requires actively growing cells that are typically obtained from marrow or blood. In the context of cancer treatment, bone marrow can be damaged by the disease itself or by therapies such as chemotherapy or radiation. It is also the target of stem cell rescue procedures, where healthy blood-forming stem cells are harvested from the marrow, and it serves as the site where new cells grow following a transplant. Biopsies of bone marrow are commonly performed to confirm diagnoses such as follicular lymphoma (FL) and to assess disease involvement in various blood cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-067&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-119&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-168&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
