    <node id="Myeloblasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloblasts are immature blood cells that originate from myeloid progenitor cells in the bone marrow. Under normal conditions, they are very immature white blood cells that mature into non-lymphoid blood cells, specifically differentiating into granulocytes (such as neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils) or monocytes. As a type of blast cell, they represent an early stage in the myeloid cell lineage.

In pathological contexts, myeloblasts play a central role in acute myeloid leukemia (AML). In AML, these immature white blood cells become abnormal and accumulate in the bone marrow and blood, preventing normal blood cell production and function. Similarly, myeloblasts are identified as a type of blast cell involved in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), where their presence reflects disrupted myeloid maturation. Across these conditions, the key characteristic of myeloblasts is their immaturity and their failure to undergo normal differentiation, leading to their accumulation and impairment of healthy hematopoiesis.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682919</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
