    <node id="Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of lung cancer originating from lung cells, with several subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682920</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The lungs are a pair of organs located in the chest, forming a critical component of the respiratory system. Their primary physiological function is to facilitate gas exchange, moving oxygen into the blood and carbon dioxide out of it, and they are the essential organs responsible for breathing. Beyond their normal function, the lungs are a significant clinical focus in oncology, serving as a frequent and common site for distant metastasis for numerous primary cancers. Specifically, they are a well-documented destination for cancer spread from the throat, rectum, kidney, pancreas, prostate, breast, colon, cervix, stomach, bladder, and anus. In the context of specific cancers, the lungs are a common site for metastasis in stage 4 colon cancer, a potential site for spread in nasopharyngeal and stomach cancers, and a frequent target for metastatic cervical, anal, and pancreatic cancers. Additionally, the lungs are the primary site where small cell lung cancer (SCLC) tumors can grow and spread. Due to their susceptibility to metastatic involvement, the lungs are routinely examined via chest imaging to detect potential cancer spread. While they are a common metastatic target for many malignancies, they are also an organ to which cancer cells less frequently spread in certain other contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682923</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Large Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Large Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A subtype of NSCLC that forms from large cells in the airways.&lt;SEP&gt;A subtype of NSCLC that starts in large cells and can grow anywhere in the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;A subtype of non-small cell lung cancer that starts in large cells and can grow anywhere in the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682923</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a broad category of cancer that originates in glandular cells, which are responsible for producing and secreting substances such as mucus. This cancer type can arise in various organs, and its characteristics and prevalence differ depending on the location. In the lungs, adenocarcinoma is a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) that begins in the mucus-making cells lining the alveoli. It is the most common subtype of NSCLC. In the colorectal region, adenocarcinoma is the most frequent type of tumor, forming within adenomas (precancerous polyps) and representing the most common form of both colon and rectal cancer. It also accounts for the vast majority of colorectal cancers overall, distinguishing it from cancers arising from squamous cells.

Adenocarcinoma also occurs in other parts of the digestive and reproductive systems. In the stomach, it is the most common type of cancer, developing from the mucus-secreting cells that line the stomach wall; indeed, nearly all stomach cancers are adenocarcinomas. In the cervix, adenocarcinoma is a recognized type of cervical cancer that arises from the glandular cells located in the endocervix, and it is considered a common form of this disease. Additionally, adenocarcinoma can appear in the bladder, where it is a less common, non-urothelial type of cancer. Across all these sites, the defining feature of adenocarcinoma is its origin in glandular tissue, which influences its behavior, diagnosis, and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-141&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682927</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early and Locally Advanced NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Early and Locally Advanced NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancers that have not spread to the lining around the lung or other organs, including stages 1, 2, and 3.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682927</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a type of cancer that arises from squamous cells, which are thin, flat cells found in tissues forming the surface of the skin, mucous membranes, and the lining of various organs and airways. Because these cells are widespread, squamous cell carcinoma can develop in multiple anatomical sites, and its characteristics, prevalence, and staging vary depending on the location.

In the context of lung cancer, squamous cell carcinoma is a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) that originates in the squamous cells lining the bronchi or airways. In the head and neck region, it is the most common type of mouth cancer, arising from the squamous cells lining the mucous membranes inside the mouth, and it is also the most common type of throat cancer, forming in the thin, flat cells of the mucus membranes. For throat cancer, staging information for this type is provided when the tumor tests negative for p16. Additionally, it is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer, forming in the squamous cells lining the nasopharynx, and the TNM staging system is specifically applied to this type of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.

Squamous cell carcinoma is also the most common type of cervical cancer, arising from squamous cells in the ectocervix. In the bladder, it represents a less common, non-urothelial type of cancer. Finally, it is the most common type of anal cancer, originating in the squamous cells that form the surface of the skin and other tissues, and it is the focus of certain patient guides for anal cancer. Overall, while the fundamental origin in squamous cells is consistent, the clinical significance, frequency, and management of squamous cell carcinoma are highly dependent on the primary site of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-141&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
