    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stage">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Stage is a medical classification that describes the extent of cancer in the body, often written with Roman numerals (e.g., I, II, III, IV). It serves as a standardized assessment of how far the cancer has likely spread from its original site, such as the ovary, and is a critical factor in determining the appropriate treatment plan for a patient. The staging system categorizes the disease based on whether cancer has remained localized or has metastasized; specifically, stages I, II, and III are considered non-metastatic, indicating that the cancer is confined to the primary site or has spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes, while stage IV denotes metastatic cancer, meaning it has spread to distant organs or parts of the body. This classification provides a clear framework for healthcare providers to evaluate the severity of the disease, guide therapeutic decisions, and communicate the prognosis, ensuring that the treatment approach is tailored to the specific progression of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682932</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Cancer is a term used to describe cancer that has spread from its original (primary) site to other, distant parts of the body. This process of spread can involve various organs and tissues, with common distant sites including the lungs, liver, bones, and brain, as well as distant lymph nodes. The condition is often characterized as very advanced disease, and when cancer is identified as stage 4 at the time of diagnosis, it is considered metastatic from the outset. In specific contexts, such as esophageal cancer, the metastatic stage specifically denotes that the disease has spread to distant bodily locations. Regardless of the primary tumor's origin, the defining feature of metastatic cancer is its presence in areas far removed from the initial site of development.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">Chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation is a treatment strategy that combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy, typically administered concurrently to enhance therapeutic efficacy. This approach is utilized across a range of cancer types, particularly for locally advanced or non-metastatic diseases. In the context of locally advanced cervical cancer, chemoradiation is recommended for most cases and involves the concurrent use of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) alongside chemotherapy, often with a platinum-based regimen. Similarly, for most non-metastatic anal cancers and perianal cancers that do not meet criteria for local excision, chemoradiation is the standard recommended treatment. It is also employed in the management of bladder cancer, where chemotherapy and radiation therapy are used together. In cases of metastatic anal cancer, chemoradiation may be administered after initial chemotherapy, though it is not generally considered following a modified DCF (docetaxel, cisplatin, and fluorouracil) regimen. The treatment is broadly defined as the combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy, with specific applications varying by cancer type and stage. For anal cancer, clinical discussions often include considerations of chemotherapy regimens and radiation therapy details. Overall, chemoradiation serves as a cornerstone in the multidisciplinary management of several malignancies, aiming to improve local control and overall outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-141&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-183&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-193&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-197</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682936</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Supportive Care">
      <data key="d0">Supportive Care</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive Care is a specialized area of healthcare designed to address the symptoms, side effects, and challenges associated with cancer and its treatment, with the primary goal of extending and enhancing life for patients. It is an integral part of cancer care, applicable to conditions such as lung cancer and chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), and is available at any stage of the disease. This form of care focuses on relieving symptoms caused by the cancer itself as well as the side effects of treatments, aiming to improve comfort and overall quality of life. It is not intended to treat the cancer directly, but rather to manage physical, emotional, and practical needs, including pain management and emotional support. Supportive care is always an option for patients, providing a holistic approach that helps individuals cope with the multifaceted challenges of their illness and treatment journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-145&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-184</data>
