    <node id="Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A subtype of cancer that starts in cells lining the airways; almost all lung cancers are carcinomas.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that starts in cells lining inner or outer surfaces of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that starts in cells lining the inner or outer surfaces of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682938</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical Trials are health research studies designed to test new ways of fighting cancer, including new treatments, therapies, and procedures. These studies investigate the safety and effectiveness of investigational treatments, with a primary focus on advancing medical care and offering hope to patients, such as those with pancreatic cancer. In the context of specific diseases, clinical trials are used to test new treatments or procedures for conditions like Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and are being conducted in large numbers to find more targeted treatments for gliomas. Biomarker testing can determine a patient's eligibility for certain trials, and circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) testing is currently being evaluated within these research settings. By testing new therapies, clinical trials help advance treatment options for everyone, contributing to broader progress in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-109&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-168</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682940</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Histology">
      <data key="d0">Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Histology is the branch of biology and medicine concerned with the study of the microscopic structure of tissues, including their cells and anatomy, as well as the structure of organs under a microscope. In clinical practice, histology involves the detailed review of the appearance and cellular composition of tissue samples. This discipline is fundamental to pathology, where it is used to classify cancer subtypes—such as renal cell carcinoma (RCC)—by examining tissue under a microscope. Additionally, histology is employed to determine tumor grade and assess tumor grade, which informs prognosis and guides treatment decisions. The classification of tumors based on the microscopic appearance of cells and tissues is a core application of histology, making it essential for accurate diagnosis and therapeutic planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682942</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN.org is the official website of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN), serving as a central, authoritative online resource where the organization’s clinical practice guidelines are made publicly accessible. The site is specifically dedicated to hosting the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which are designed to provide clear, evidence-based information to individuals affected by cancer. These patient guidelines cover a broad spectrum of oncology topics, including general cancer care and specific information on lung cancer screening. Notably, the website also contains dedicated patient guidelines for Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC), offering detailed guidance on that particular disease. As the digital home of the NCCN, the platform functions as the primary portal through which both patients and the public can locate and review these essential, expert-developed recommendations and educational materials.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-093&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682944</data>
