    <node id="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in breast tissue, most commonly originating in the epithelial cells of the terminal duct lobular units, specifically within the ducts or lobules. The vast majority of breast cancers are carcinomas. This malignant disease can spread to surrounding tissue and other parts of the body, such as the lungs; when it metastasizes, it is still referred to as breast cancer.

The condition is frequently associated with germline mutations, particularly in the BRCA genes, and can be hereditary in approximately 1 out of 10 cases. A personal or family history of breast cancer may also be linked to an increased risk of developing other cancers, such as prostate cancer and thyroid cancer. Clinical discussions of breast cancer often focus on staging, prognostic factors, and the use of biomarker testing and tumor markers for diagnosis and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
