    <node id="Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A muscular tube that carries food from the throat to the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;A muscular tube connecting the throat to the stomach. It has four layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria, and tunica adventitia. The upper part has striated muscle, the middle has a mixture, and the lower part has smooth muscle.&lt;SEP&gt;A hollow muscular tube that carries food and liquid to the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;The swallowing tube leading to the stomach, examined during esophagoscopy and panendoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;The tube that goes to the stomach, into which the pharynx opens.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the digestive tract that connects the mouth to the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;A muscular tube that connects the throat to the stomach, through which food and drink pass.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach">
      <data key="d0">Stomach</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Organ where food passes from the esophagus and is broken down.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ that meets the esophagus at the esophagogastric junction, containing acid.&lt;SEP&gt;An organ near the liver.&lt;SEP&gt;A muscular, hollow organ that secretes enzymes and acid to aid digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;A digestive organ that receives food from the esophagus, mixes it with enzymes and acid, and releases chyme into the small intestine. It has parts including cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">System that transports and breaks down food, absorbs nutrients, and removes waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The system that takes in food, breaks it down, absorbs nutrients, and removes waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The system in the body that takes in and breaks down food, absorbs nutrients, and removes waste. It includes organs such as the esophagus, stomach, small intestine, colon, and rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;The bodily system responsible for breaking down food, absorbing nutrients, and removing waste.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that starts when abnormal cells grow out of control in the layers of the esophagus inner lining.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that starts in the epithelium of the esophagus when cells grow out of control. It can spread to nearby lymph nodes, veins, arteries, and organs such as the liver, pancreas, lung, and spleen. It may also grow into lymphatic or blood vessels and spread to distant parts. Stages include early-stage, locoregional, and metastatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer of the esophagus, which can show p16 expression.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that affects the esophagus, mentioned as not to be confused with nasopharyngeal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that starts in the esophagus or at the esophagogastric junction; tumors that start in the cardia and cross into the junction are treated as esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inner membrane of the esophagus wall that contacts food.&lt;SEP&gt;The innermost layer of the esophagus where cancer usually starts. Precancerous changes often occur here.&lt;SEP&gt;The innermost layer of the colon wall that comes in contact with food.&lt;SEP&gt;The innermost layer of the stomach wall, in contact with food, consisting of surface epithelium, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosae.&lt;SEP&gt;The innermost layer of the stomach wall where most stomach cancers begin.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Submucosa">
      <data key="d0">Submucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Layer of connective tissue below the mucosa containing blood vessels, nerves, and lymphatic vessels.&lt;SEP&gt;A layer of the esophagus wall beneath the mucosa.&lt;SEP&gt;The layer of the colon wall made of connective tissue containing mucus glands, blood and lymph vessels, and nerves.&lt;SEP&gt;A layer of connective tissue, blood vessels, and nerve cells beneath the mucosa.&lt;SEP&gt;The second layer of the stomach wall, which cancer may invade in locoregional stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muscularis Propria">
      <data key="d0">Muscularis Propria</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Thick muscle layer under the submucosa that contracts to push food down.&lt;SEP&gt;A muscle layer of the esophagus wall.&lt;SEP&gt;The muscle layer of the colon wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tunica Adventitia">
      <data key="d0">Tunica Adventitia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Outermost layer of the esophagus wall made of connective tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;The outermost layer of the esophagus, made of connective tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peristalsis">
      <data key="d0">Peristalsis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Coordinated contractions that move food down the esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sphincters">
      <data key="d0">Sphincters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Circular muscles that normally remain closed except during swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Interventional Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Interventional Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Specialist who may be part of the assessment team for lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A specialist who uses imaging guidance to perform minimally invasive procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;A physician who performs needle biopsies and places IV ports.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682946</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor is an abnormal mass or growth of tissue that arises from the uncontrolled overgrowth of cells, and it can be either benign (non-cancerous) or malignant (cancerous). In the context of cancer, a tumor specifically refers to the primary cancerous mass, which is a key element in staging and treatment planning. Tumors can develop in various parts of the body, including lymph nodes, the prostate, the bladder, bones, the throat, and the ovaries, where they may present as solid or fluid-filled masses. Their size is often measured in centimeters or millimeters, and this measurement, along with the extent of local invasion into nearby structures, is used to assign a T score in cancer staging systems. For example, primary bone tumors are staged based on size and location, while throat and bladder tumors are assessed for their potential to invade deeper tissue layers or spread. Tumors may be detected as irregular or hardened areas during physical examination, such as in the prostate, or identified through medical procedures. Biopsy samples are commonly taken from tumors to determine whether they are cancerous, and in many cases, tumors can be surgically removed. Overall, the term tumor encompasses a wide range of abnormal growths, with its clinical significance largely determined by its benign or malignant nature, size, location, and potential for invasion or metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682948</data>
