    <node id="Digestive Tract">
      <data key="d0">Digestive Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Part of the digestive system, also called the gastrointestinal (GI) tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682948</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth">
      <data key="d0">Mouth</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Entry point for food and drink into the digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;The oral cavity, first part of the digestive and respiratory systems, involved in eating and speaking.&lt;SEP&gt;Oral contrast is taken by mouth.&lt;SEP&gt;The oral cavity, a site for HPV infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Oral cavity examined during panendoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682948</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Small Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Small Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Part of the digestive system where food enters after being broken down.&lt;SEP&gt;The part of the digestive system where bile is released to help digest food.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the digestive tract where nutrients are absorbed.&lt;SEP&gt;The part of the digestive system where nutrients from chyme are absorbed into the bloodstream.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ used in urinary diversions; a segment may be used to create a neobladder or connect a pouch to a stoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682948</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Large Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Large Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Part of the digestive system that prepares unused food to be moved out of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The bowel, consisting of the colon and rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the digestive tract that prepares unused food for removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ used to create a pouch in continent cutaneous urinary diversion.&lt;SEP&gt;The part of the digestive system that includes the colon and rectum. The anus is at its very end.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682948</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical Oncologist is a physician and medical specialist who treats cancer using systemic therapy, which includes chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy, among other medications. As a core member of the multidisciplinary care team, this doctor may be part of the assessment team for various cancers, including lung cancer, and is also a key specialist on the thyroid cancer treatment team, where they manage systemic cancer treatments. Beyond administering these therapies, the medical oncologist often coordinates overall patient care, working alongside other specialists to ensure comprehensive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-073&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-148&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-150&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682950</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreas">
      <data key="d0">Pancreas</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Organ in the digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ that esophageal cancer can spread to.&lt;SEP&gt;A large gland in the digestive system that makes enzymes and hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;A gland that helps digest food and control blood sugar, where pancreatic cancer originates.&lt;SEP&gt;An organ near the liver, sometimes considered part of the hepatobiliary system.&lt;SEP&gt;An organ in the digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;An organ that produces digestive enzymes and hormones such as insulin.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682950</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liver">
      <data key="d0">Liver</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The liver is a large organ located in the abdomen, on the right side of the body under the rib cage, and is a central component of the digestive system. It performs numerous vital functions, including filtering blood to remove toxins, producing bile to break down fats, processing nutrients and sugar for energy, synthesizing coagulation factors to aid in blood clotting, and producing lymph. Its health and function are commonly assessed through blood tests, such as a chemistry profile or liver function tests, and it can be imaged using MRI to detect signs of disease.

The liver is a frequent and clinically significant site for cancer metastasis. It is a common destination for cancer cells originating from various primary tumors, including breast, colon, rectal, anal, stomach, pancreatic, cervical, bladder, kidney, prostate, throat, and nasopharyngeal cancers. In many of these cases, such as with colon cancer, the liver is a primary site for stage 4 spread, while for others, like kidney cancer, it is a less frequent but still possible site of distant metastasis. The liver can also be affected by leukemia spreading outside the bloodstream, and cancer cell buildup in the organ can cause it to swell.

Beyond its role in metastasis, the liver is susceptible to damage from certain medical treatments, such as high-dose methotrexate. As an organ that processes chemicals, its function is critical to overall health, and its assessment is a standard part of medical diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-078&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-118&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682953</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colon">
      <data key="d0">Colon</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The colon, also known as the large intestine, is a crucial part of the digestive tract and the digestive system. It is the first and longest section of the large bowel, playing a primary role in the final stages of digestion. Functionally, the colon is responsible for absorbing water from digested food, a process that transforms the remaining material into solid stool. It is the specific part of the large intestine where digested food becomes stool.

Anatomically, the colon is the segment of the large intestine that may be surgically connected to the abdominal wall in a procedure called a colostomy. This connection allows for the diversion of waste when normal bowel function is compromised.

Medically, the colon is a significant site for disease, particularly colon cancer. It is the part of the large intestine where this cancer typically develops. Furthermore, cancer originating in the colon can spread (metastasize) to other organs, most notably the liver, making it a critical area for oncological monitoring and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682955</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectum">
      <data key="d0">Rectum</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectum is the final section of the large intestine, connecting the colon to the anus, and is the last part of the large bowel. It is located in the pelvis and measures approximately the last several inches (about 5 inches) of the large intestine. As part of the digestive system and lower digestive tract, its primary function is to store stool (feces) until it leaves the body through the anus. The rectum is situated immediately above the anal canal.

In medical contexts, the rectum is clinically significant. It is examined together with the vagina during a rectovaginal exam. Additionally, ultrasound probes and biopsy needles may be inserted through the rectum for prostate procedures, such as a prostate biopsy, and bleeding may occur in this area following such a biopsy. The rectum is also a site where polyps can form. Surgically, it may be removed during a pelvic exenteration or an abdominoperineal resection (APR), as it is the lower part of the large intestine targeted in these procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-161&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682956</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spine">
      <data key="d0">Spine</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The backbone, in front of which the esophagus is located.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the body to which throat cancer cells may spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the bone where breast cancer can metastasize.&lt;SEP&gt;The vertebral column; a common site for breast cancer bone metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The vertebral column, where chordomas are usually found in the lower part or at the base of the skull.&lt;SEP&gt;The spinal or vertebral column, including vertebrae, sacrum, and coccyx.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682956</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Throat">
      <data key="d0">Throat</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Part of the body through which food passes to the esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;A hollow, muscular tube inside the neck that starts behind the nose and ends at the top of the trachea and esophagus. It acts as a passageway for air, food, and liquid, and contains structures such as the tonsils, pharynx, and larynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the body where throat cancer occurs, including the oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682956</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anus">
      <data key="d0">Anus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The anus is the opening at the end of the digestive tract through which stool (or feces) exits the body. It is located in the pelvis at the very end of the large intestine (large bowel). This anatomical structure serves as the terminal point of the gastrointestinal system and is the site where anal cancer originates. Additionally, the anus is a potential site for HPV infection. In certain medical procedures, such as pelvic exenteration, the anus may be surgically removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nerves">
      <data key="d0">Nerves</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Structures in the submucosa that transmit signals.&lt;SEP&gt;Nerve tissue present in the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Nerve tissue in the breast that provides sensation.&lt;SEP&gt;Nerve fibers present in the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Structures in the submucosa that carry lymph.&lt;SEP&gt;Vessels that cancer can grow into and spread through.&lt;SEP&gt;Ducts that transport lymph, a clear fluid carrying lymphocytes, throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Thin tubes that carry lymph fluid and white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Channels through which lymph fluid drains into lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Vessels that carry lymph, part of the immune system; cancer can spread through them.&lt;SEP&gt;A network of thin tubes that carry lymphatic fluid and white blood cells into all the tissues of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Glands">
      <data key="d0">Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Structures in some parts of the esophagus that secrete mucus.&lt;SEP&gt;Organs in the neck area that may show symptoms of head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mucus">
      <data key="d0">Mucus</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Substance secreted by glands in the esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;A sticky, thick liquid produced by the stomach lining that protects the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagogastric Junction">
      <data key="d0">Esophagogastric Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The junction where the esophagus meets the stomach, located beneath the diaphragm. Also called the gastroesophageal junction.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diaphragm">
      <data key="d0">Diaphragm</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle beneath which the esophagogastric junction is located.&lt;SEP&gt;A barrier contraceptive device placed over the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;A thin muscle below the lungs and heart that separates the chest from the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of esophageal cancer that starts in the thin, flat squamous cells lining the esophagus. Often found in the upper and middle esophagus but can occur in the lower esophagus. More common in Eastern Europe, Asia, and Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of esophageal cancer that starts in the mucus-making cells of the inner lining. Often found in the lower esophagus but can occur in the middle esophagus. More common in North America and Western Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Barrett Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Barrett Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">A condition where the squamous cells lining the lower esophagus are replaced with abnormal cells due to chronic reflux. Increases the risk of developing esophageal adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Blood Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Vessels are tubular structures that carry blood throughout the body, forming a network that extends into nearly all tissues and organs. They are present in various anatomical locations, including within the submucosa layer of organs and specifically within the breast tissue, where they supply blood to the breast and carry blood through it. Functionally, these vessels are critical for circulation, but they also play a significant role in disease, particularly cancer. Cancer cells can grow into these vessels and travel through them, using the bloodstream as a route to spread (metastasize) to other parts of the body. This ability to invade blood vessels is a key mechanism of cancer progression, and such vessels are found not only in the submucosa but also in other tissue layers, providing multiple pathways for potential dissemination.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682960</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lung">
      <data key="d0">Lung</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung is an organ in the respiratory system that serves as a frequent and clinically significant site for cancer metastasis. It is a common destination for distant metastasis originating from several primary cancers, including breast cancer, where it is a well-documented site of spread, and esophageal cancer, to which it is particularly susceptible. Additionally, the lung can be affected by metastatic colon cancer and is also a common site for bone cancer metastasis. Furthermore, it is a target organ for metastasis from liver cancer. As a respiratory organ, the lung’s role in metastasis underscores its vulnerability to secondary tumor growth from various malignancies, making it a critical focus in oncology for monitoring and treating advanced cancer stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682962</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spleen">
      <data key="d0">Spleen</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The spleen is an organ located in the abdomen, near the liver, and is a key component of the lymphatic system. As the largest organ of the lymphatic system, it measures about 4 inches long and performs several vital functions, including the production of lymphocytes, blood filtration, and blood storage. Its role in the immune system helps the body fight infections, though it can become enlarged or swollen due to the accumulation of cancer cells.

In the context of various cancers, the spleen is a notable site for disease spread or manifestation. Esophageal cancer, stomach cancer, and leukemia can all spread to the spleen, with leukemia capable of extending there outside the bloodstream. Additionally, the spleen may be enlarged in follicular lymphoma, and tumors associated with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) can be found within it. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) or small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL) cells can also cause the spleen to swell, reflecting its involvement in these hematologic malignancies. Overall, the spleen’s dual role in immunity and blood regulation makes it susceptible to infiltration by multiple cancer types, leading to splenomegaly as a common clinical sign.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682964</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone">
      <data key="d0">Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone is a specialized connective tissue that forms the skeleton, characterized as a hard yet living tissue composed of bone cells, collagen, blood vessels, nerves, proteins, vitamins, and minerals. It is light and strong, capable of regrowing and undergoing continuous remodeling, and it also produces hormones. As a dynamic tissue, bone can be damaged by cancer, leading to complications such as calcium release into the body.

In the context of oncology, bone is a frequent and clinically significant site for metastasis across multiple cancer types. It is a common site for distant metastasis in breast cancer, including spread to the spine, and is also a primary target for prostate cancer metastasis, often detected via bone scans. Additionally, bone serves as a common site for metastatic esophageal cancer, cervical cancer, liver cancer, and stomach cancer. When cancer spreads to bone, affected areas may appear as bright spots on imaging scans due to increased tracer absorption, indicating active metastatic lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-094&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-155&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-163</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682966</data>
