    </node>
    <node id="Classic Follicular Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Classic Follicular Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">A subtype of follicular lymphoma defined by grade, treated as indolent.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682976</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of lymphoma with germinal centers different from those in follicular lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is essentially the same as Burkitt leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma, essentially the same as Burkitt leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is essentially the same as Burkitt leukemia but treated differently.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682976</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Fluid">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Fluid</data>
      <data key="d1">biologicalfluid</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluid that travels through the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682976</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Antibody">
      <data key="d0">Antibody</data>
      <data key="d1">protein</data>
      <data key="d2">A protein that targets infections or cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A protein made by B lymphocytes that helps fight infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682976</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade">
      <data key="d0">Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification of follicular lymphoma based on cell types, ranging from 1 to 3B.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of how abnormal tumor cells look under a microscope; higher grades indicate faster growth and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Describes how abnormal tumor cells look under a microscope; higher grades grow and spread faster.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of how abnormal tumor cells look under a microscope, indicating aggressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;A rating of how abnormal tumor cells are, indicating the likely growth rate and spread of the tumor, ranging from 1 to 4.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of how fast cancer is growing, used to predict disease course and plan treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A rating of the aggressiveness of cancer, estimating how fast it is likely to grow and spread. Grades are either low grade (slow) or high grade (fast).</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682976</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage">
      <data key="d0">Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The extent of cancer spread, determined by grade and other factors.&lt;SEP&gt;A rating of the extent of cancer before treatment, used to determine treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of how far cancer has grown or spread, used to predict disease course and plan treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A rating system used to describe the physical extent of cancer, indicating how far it has grown into the bladder wall and spread to other parts of the body. Stages range from 0 to 4.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682976</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle">
      <data key="d0">Muscle</data>
      <data key="d1">biologicaltissue</data>
      <data key="d2">Tissue found between the membranes of the bile duct.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682976</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic System">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Lymphatic System is a major part of the body’s immune system, functioning as a network of tissues, organs, and vessels that helps the body fight infection and disease. It is composed primarily of lymphocytes and includes key structures such as lymph nodes, the spleen, bone marrow, thymus, tonsils, and lymphatic vessels. This system also serves as a network that carries lymph—a clear fluid containing white blood cells—throughout the body, helping to circulate immune cells and defend against germs. Additionally, the lymphatic system plays a role in ridding the body of toxins and waste. Because it transports lymph fluid, cancer cells can spread through this system to other parts of the body. Overall, it is essential for immune defense, fluid balance, and waste removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-088&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-171&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammation">
      <data key="d0">Inflammation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A state that triggers germinal center formation and lymphocyte response.&lt;SEP&gt;A bodily response that can appear as bright spots on PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition that can show up as a bright spot on PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition that can appear as bright spots on PET scans, not necessarily cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition that can show up as a bright spot on a PET scan, potentially mimicking cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bacteria">
      <data key="d0">Bacteria</data>
      <data key="d1">microorganism</data>
      <data key="d2">Foreign material that can be filtered by lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Extranodal Disease">
      <data key="d0">Extranodal Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma that forms tumors outside the lymph nodes, such as in the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Disease that occurs outside the lymph nodes, a characteristic of mediastinal gray zone lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Stressor">
      <data key="d0">Immune Stressor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Something that triggers the formation of germinal centers, such as infection or inflammation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Light Zone">
      <data key="d0">Light Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomicalstructure</data>
      <data key="d2">A zone in the germinal center containing centrocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dark Zone">
      <data key="d0">Dark Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomicalstructure</data>
      <data key="d2">A zone in the germinal center containing centroblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="WHO5">
      <data key="d0">WHO5</data>
      <data key="d1">classificationsystem</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2022 World Health Organization classification for lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;World Health Organization classification system that classifies follicular lymphoma grades 1, 2, and 3A as classic FL.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ICC">
      <data key="d0">ICC</data>
      <data key="d1">classificationsystem</data>
      <data key="d2">International Consensus Classification for lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;International Consensus Classification system that groups grades 1 and 2 as classic FL, and is followed by the book.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 1">
      <data key="d0">Grade 1</data>
      <data key="d1">grade</data>
      <data key="d2">A grade of follicular lymphoma with fewer centroblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 2">
      <data key="d0">Grade 2</data>
      <data key="d1">grade</data>
      <data key="d2">A grade of follicular lymphoma with more centroblasts than grade 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3A">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3A</data>
      <data key="d1">grade</data>
      <data key="d2">A grade of follicular lymphoma with many centroblasts but still some centrocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3B">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3B</data>
      <data key="d1">grade</data>
      <data key="d2">A grade of follicular lymphoma containing only centroblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Classic FL">
      <data key="d0">Classic FL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of follicular lymphoma that includes grades 1, 2, and sometimes 3A depending on classification system. It is slow-growing and often associated with BCL2 protein overexpression or chromosome translocation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 1 FL">
      <data key="d0">Grade 1 FL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A grade of follicular lymphoma that is considered classic FL under both WHO5 and ICC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 2 FL">
      <data key="d0">Grade 2 FL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A grade of follicular lymphoma that is considered classic FL under both WHO5 and ICC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3A FL">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3A FL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A grade of follicular lymphoma that may be treated as classic FL or DLBCL depending on cancer center and cell types.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3B FL">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3B FL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare grade of follicular lymphoma, also called follicular large B cell lymphoma in WHO5, treated as DLBCL.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DLBCL">
      <data key="d0">DLBCL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, an aggressive lymphoma that FL can transform into.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCL2 Protein">
      <data key="d0">BCL2 Protein</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A protein that is often overexpressed in follicular lymphoma tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic abnormality involving the switching of part of one chromosome with another, common in follicular lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Switching of parts between two chromosomes, e.g., t(8;21)(q22;q1).</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uncommon FL">
      <data key="d0">Uncommon FL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma with uncommon pathologic features or t(14;18)-negative, treated as classic FL in the book.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric-type FL">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric-type FL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of follicular lymphoma that usually occurs in children but can occur in adults, often with only one enlarged lymph node in head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transformed FL">
      <data key="d0">Transformed FL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma that has transformed into a more aggressive lymphoma, typically DLBCL.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">B Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of lymphoma including fever, night sweats, fatigue, and weight loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of CLL including fevers, night sweats, and weight loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682978</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Infection">
      <data key="d0">Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Infection is a biological state characterized by the invasion of the body by harmful microorganisms, such as pathogens, which the body actively fights using immune cells. This immune response can trigger the formation of germinal centers and stimulate a lymphocyte response, indicating an active defense mechanism. In medical imaging, an infection can manifest as a bright spot on PET scans, a finding that is not necessarily indicative of cancer but must be considered in differential diagnosis. Additionally, infection represents a potential complication of invasive procedures, particularly prostate biopsy and other biopsy types, where the introduction of harmful microorganisms can lead to localized or systemic illness.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682979</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Large B Cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Large B Cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternative name for follicular lymphoma grade 3B (FL3B) in the WHO5 classification, which is rare and treated as DLBCL.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682979</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="t(14;18)">
      <data key="d0">t(14;18)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific chromosome translocation often associated with follicular lymphoma; its absence may lead to classification as uncommon FL.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682979</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotyping">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotyping</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A test that identifies the type of cells in a sample, used in diagnosing follicular lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A process using flow cytometry to identify markers on cells, particularly in the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;A process that uses antibodies to detect the presence or absence of white blood cell antigens called biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;A process using antibodies to detect white blood cell antigens.&lt;SEP&gt;A test that identifies cells based on the types of antigens or markers on the cell surface.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682979</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Mouth Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth Cancer, also known as oral cancer or oral cavity cancer, is a malignant condition that develops in any part of the mouth, including the lips, buccal mucosa (inner cheek lining), tongue, gums, floor of the mouth, hard palate, and retromolar trigone. It is classified as a type of head and neck cancer and most commonly originates in the squamous cells, making it typically a squamous cell carcinoma of the mucosa of the lip and other oral surfaces. The disease is a recognized topic in medical literature and is referenced in resources such as the NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) guidelines, which provide further information on its management.

Clinically, Mouth Cancer is staged using the TNM (Tumor, Node, Metastasis) system, with stages ranging from 0 to 4. This staging process is crucial as it directly guides treatment decisions, helping clinicians determine the appropriate therapeutic approach based on the extent of the disease. However, from a molecular perspective, testing for this cancer has limited value due to the scarcity of targeted drugs available for its treatment, which contrasts with other cancer types where molecular profiling may offer more actionable therapeutic options. Overall, Mouth Cancer encompasses a range of malignancies within the oral cavity, with its diagnosis, staging, and treatment protocols being well-documented in oncological practice.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682982</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility is the ability to conceive or have children, a consideration that applies to individuals of all genders. In the context of cancer care, fertility is a significant health concern because cancer treatments—including chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and other modalities—can adversely affect a person’s reproductive capacity. This impact is relevant across various cancer types, such as follicular lymphoma, head and neck cancer, acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), acute myeloid leukemia (AML), and anal cancer, among others.

For patients, fertility is an important topic to discuss with their healthcare team before and during treatment planning. Such discussions allow for proactive measures, including fertility testing for all genders, to assess and preserve reproductive potential. The potential for treatment-related infertility means that fertility considerations should be integrated into the overall care strategy, ensuring patients are informed about risks and available options. Across different cancer diagnoses, the core message remains consistent: cancer treatment may affect fertility, and addressing this concern is a vital part of comprehensive patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-069&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-154&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-172&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788682985</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Tests">
      <data key="d0">Heart Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart Tests are medical procedures used to assess the function of the heart. These cardiac evaluations are designed to determine how well the heart works, which is crucial for patients undergoing cancer treatment. Specifically, these tests may be required before certain treatments for conditions such as Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) or Follicular Lymphoma (FL), as well as other cancer therapies. Beyond pre-treatment assessment, Heart Tests are also utilized to monitor potential side effects of ongoing treatment and to measure baseline heart function. In summary, Heart Tests serve the dual purpose of evaluating cardiovascular health prior to medical interventions and tracking the heart's condition throughout the course of treatment to ensure patient safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
