    <node id="Distress Screening">
      <data key="d0">Distress Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress Screening is a structured process used in cancer care to assess a patient’s level of emotional and psychological distress, which may include feelings of sadness, fear, anxiety, and other emotional responses to a cancer diagnosis. It functions as a screening tool or assessment method designed to identify emotional or psychological distress in patients, specifically those diagnosed with cancer. The process involves the treatment team evaluating the patient’s distress, which is understood as an unpleasant experience that can be of a mental, physical, social, or spiritual nature. As an integral part of cancer care, Distress Screening aims to systematically detect and evaluate the presence and severity of distress, thereby enabling appropriate follow-up and support for affected patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-128</data>
