    <node id="Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Plan is a comprehensive, personalized strategy for treating cancer, developed by a multidisciplinary care team in collaboration with the patient. It is primarily determined by diagnostic results, including imaging studies, biopsies, and blood tests, which help establish the cancer's stage and grade. The plan is designed to guide the selection of the most appropriate therapy, taking into account critical factors such as the patient's performance status and personal preferences. It may be tailored for specific conditions, such as acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where the approach is chosen jointly by the patient and care team based on test outcomes. The plan can be highly specific, detailing elements like radiation doses and the number of treatment sessions, or it can be a broader proposed course of medical treatment. Ultimately, the Treatment Plan is a documented agreement that outlines the agreed-upon cancer treatment, potential side effects, therapeutic goals, and other essential care details, ensuring all parties are aligned on the path forward.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-120&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-179&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683024</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TNM System">
      <data key="d0">TNM System</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM System is a standardized cancer staging system used to describe the anatomical extent of a malignant disease. It evaluates three key components: the primary tumor (T), the involvement of regional lymph nodes (N), and the presence of distant metastasis (M). These categories are assessed and combined to assign an overall cancer stage, which helps guide prognosis and treatment decisions.

This system is applied across various cancer types, including mouth cancer, throat cancer, and most bone cancers. For each specific cancer, the T, N, and M scores are defined to reflect the unique patterns of local growth, lymphatic spread, and distant dissemination. The TNM System provides a common language for clinicians to describe tumor size, extent of spread, and the presence of secondary growths, ensuring consistent and objective disease classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683027</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Stage">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical Stage (c) is a rating or classification of cancer assigned before any treatment is administered. It is determined through a combination of physical exams, biopsies, and imaging tests, such as X-rays or scans, and serves as a pre-treatment estimate of how far the cancer has spread. This staging is based on non-surgical findings, meaning it does not incorporate information obtained from surgical procedures. It is also considered a prognostic stage, used to guide initial treatment decisions and predict outcomes. The clinical stage is established prior to surgery and relies on the results of physical examinations and imaging, without including pathological data from operative findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683028</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic Stage, also known as surgical stage or pathologic stage (p), is the stage of cancer determined by examining tissue removed during a surgical procedure. This assessment is performed after the surgery has taken place, distinguishing it from clinical staging, which is based on examinations conducted before treatment. The pathologic stage is widely regarded as providing the most accurate picture of the disease's extent, as it is based on direct analysis of the actual removed tissue rather than imaging or physical exams alone.

This staging method offers more precise and detailed information regarding key prognostic factors, particularly the size of the primary tumor and the status of lymph nodes. By evaluating the tissue excised during operations such as lymph node removal, physicians can determine the exact degree of cancer spread. Consequently, the pathologic stage serves as a critical prognostic stage, informing treatment decisions and providing a more reliable basis for predicting patient outcomes than other staging methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-106&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-107&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683031</data>
