    <node id="Cardiologist">
      <data key="d0">Cardiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A heart specialist to whom patients might be referred for heart tests.&lt;SEP&gt;A heart specialist to whom patients may be referred for heart tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lumbar puncture, also known as a spinal tap, is a medical procedure that involves inserting a needle into the lower back to remove cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) for diagnostic testing. The primary purpose of this procedure is to collect a sample of CSF, which can then be analyzed in a laboratory. It is frequently used to check for the presence of leukemia cells in the central nervous system (CNS) and spinal fluid, as well as to detect other cancer cells that may have spread to the CNS. By examining the collected fluid, healthcare providers can identify abnormalities, confirm a diagnosis, or monitor the effectiveness of treatment for conditions affecting the brain and spinal cord. The procedure is performed by a trained medical professional and is considered a standard diagnostic tool in neurology and oncology.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-068&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683038</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="QT Interval">
      <data key="d0">QT Interval</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A measure of the time between heartbeats; a prolonged corrected QT interval can cause dangerous heart rhythms.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683038</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Human Cells">
      <data key="d0">Human Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Normal human cells contain 23 pairs of chromosomes for a total of 46 chromosomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Basic structural units of the human body, containing 46 chromosomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052</data>
