    <node id="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PET/CT Scan is a combined imaging modality that integrates Positron Emission Tomography (PET) and Computed Tomography (CT) into a single procedure. This fusion allows for the simultaneous acquisition of both functional and anatomical images, providing a comprehensive view of the body's internal structures and metabolic activity. The PET component typically involves the use of a radioactive drug (tracer) to highlight areas of high cellular activity, while the CT component produces detailed cross-sectional images of the body's anatomy. By merging these two data sets, the PET/CT Scan generates highly detailed, whole-body images that are particularly effective for detecting, localizing, and assessing the spread of cancer. This imaging technique is widely utilized in oncology, including specific applications such as evaluating prostate cancer, as it enables clinicians to identify cancer locations and determine whether the disease has metastasized to other parts of the body. The combination of functional and anatomical information enhances diagnostic accuracy and aids in treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-072&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-090&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177</data>
