    <node id="Scope">
      <data key="d0">Scope</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A long, thin tube with a light and camera used to examine internal organs.&lt;SEP&gt;A thin, tube-shaped tool with a light and camera used in scoping procedures to examine internal areas.&lt;SEP&gt;A thin, tube-shaped tool inserted into the mouth or nose to examine the throat (pharynx).</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683051</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The pelvis is the lower part of the torso, situated below the abdomen and between the hip bones. It is a basin-shaped structure that supports the spinal column and protects abdominal organs. Anatomically, it contains the reproductive organs (such as the uterus, ovaries, vagina, and prostate gland), the bladder, the rectum, and the anus, as well as pelvic lymph nodes and other associated structures. In medical contexts, the pelvis is frequently examined and imaged for conditions such as ovarian cancer, and it is a key region for assessing the local spread of cancers, including rectal, prostate, anal, and regional prostate cancer. For instance, rectal cancer can grow towards the inside of the pelvis, while anal cancer may spread to pelvic lymph nodes and other structures; in staging, the pelvis is used to describe local tumor spread, particularly for non-metastatic anal cancer, where the disease has not extended beyond this area. The pelvis is also a site where radiation treatment is targeted, which can lead to concerns about bone density in the affected region. Additionally, the pelvis can be a location for primary bone tumors, such as Ewing sarcoma, which may arise in its flat bones. Overall, the pelvis serves as a critical anatomical and clinical reference point for both supportive structure and the containment of vital pelvic organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-196</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683053</data>
