    <node id="Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Regular follow-up care after cancer treatment, including physical and pelvic exams at specified intervals.&lt;SEP&gt;Monitoring for cancer recurrence after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Regular follow-up testing after treatment to detect recurrence early.&lt;SEP&gt;Watching to see if cancer comes back after treatment or removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Regular testing after treatment to detect early signs of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Homologous recombination deficiency (HRD)">
      <data key="d0">Homologous recombination deficiency (HRD)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker indicating a defect in DNA repair, used to guide treatment in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 expression">
      <data key="d0">HER2 expression</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker related to the HER2 protein, used to guide treatment in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker indicating a high number of mutations due to defective DNA mismatch repair, used in recurrent ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition where microsatellites are more numerous than normal due to defective mismatch repair.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-156</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch repair (MMR)">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch repair (MMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A DNA repair mechanism; deficiency is a biomarker for treatment guidance in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor mutational burden (TMB)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor mutational burden (TMB)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker measuring the number of mutations in a tumor, used to guide immunotherapy decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BRAF V600E mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRAF V600E mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific gene mutation that is a biomarker for targeted therapy in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Folate receptor alpha (FRα)">
      <data key="d0">Folate receptor alpha (FRα)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker that can be targeted by mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RET mutations">
      <data key="d0">RET mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene mutations that are biomarkers for targeted therapy in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NTRK gene fusions">
      <data key="d0">NTRK gene fusions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene fusions that are biomarkers for targeted therapy in recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-resistant cancer">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-resistant cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer that does not improve or worsens during platinum-based chemotherapy, or returns within 6 months.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-sensitive cancer">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-sensitive cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer that returns more than 6 months after successful platinum-based chemotherapy, indicating responsiveness to platinum drugs.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx">
      <data key="d0">Mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An antibody drug conjugate (ADC) targeting folate receptor alpha, used for recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hypersensitivity reactions">
      <data key="d0">Hypersensitivity reactions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Allergic reactions to chemotherapy drugs like carboplatin and cisplatin, which can be life-threatening.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary cytoreductive surgery">
      <data key="d0">Secondary cytoreductive surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery to remove all visible cancer before recurrence treatment in certain circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical trial">
      <data key="d0">Clinical trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A research study testing new treatments; encouraged for patients with persistent or recurrent ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A research study that tests new treatments in people.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-077&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Throat cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that can form in the throat, specifically the oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pharynx">
      <data key="d0">Pharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Part of the body located in the back of the mouth and neck, where throat cancer can occur.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the throat that starts behind the nose and opens into the larynx and esophagus. It has three parts: nasopharynx, oropharynx, and hypopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;A hollow, muscular tube inside the neck that starts behind the nose and opens into the larynx and esophagus, with three parts: nasopharynx, oropharynx, and hypopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;The pharynx is the throat; its anatomy is relevant to nasopharyngeal cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the large bowel, specifically the colon and rectum, by inserting a flexible scope through the anus. It serves multiple purposes, including screening for abnormalities, diagnosing conditions, and enabling the removal of polyps during the examination. The procedure is also a key tool for surveillance, particularly for monitoring patients after treatment for colorectal conditions.

In the context of cancer care, Colonoscopy is recommended as a surveillance test following surgery for colon cancer. The recommended schedule for these follow-up examinations varies depending on the stage of the cancer, with intervals tailored to the individual patient's risk and clinical history. Beyond post-treatment monitoring, the procedure is routinely used for general screening and diagnostic evaluation of the lower gastrointestinal tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Maintenance therapy">
      <data key="d0">Maintenance therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment approach used after initial chemotherapy to delay cancer growth, often involving PARP inhibitors or bevacizumab.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-based chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-based chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of chemotherapy using platinum drugs like carboplatin and cisplatin, used to treat ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-platinum chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Non-platinum chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy that does not use platinum drugs, often given for platinum-resistant ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine therapy">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment option for recurrent ovarian cancer that uses hormones to slow cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment approach that targets specific molecules involved in cancer growth, such as bevacizumab or mirvetuximab.&lt;SEP&gt;A cancer treatment that targets specific molecules involved in cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-116</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Antibody drug conjugate (ADC)">
      <data key="d0">Antibody drug conjugate (ADC)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of targeted therapy that combines an antibody with a cytotoxic drug, such as mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Complete remission">
      <data key="d0">Complete remission</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The absence of signs of cancer after treatment, a prerequisite for considering platinum-sensitive recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor marker">
      <data key="d0">Tumor marker</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A substance in the blood that may indicate the presence of cancer, such as CA-125.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Head and neck cancers">
      <data key="d0">Head and neck cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of cancers that occur in the head and neck region, including throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Soft palate">
      <data key="d0">Soft palate</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The back part of the roof of the mouth, included in the oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-048</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharynx">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The upper part of the throat near the nasal cavity, one of the three parts of the pharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;A small, tube-shaped structure above the soft palate that connects the nasal cavity to the oropharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;The upper part of the throat behind the nose, connecting the nasal cavity to the pharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose, where nasopharyngeal carcinoma originates.&lt;SEP&gt;The upper part of the throat behind the nose, where nasopharyngeal carcinoma originates.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-127&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hypopharynx">
      <data key="d0">Hypopharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The lower part of the throat behind the larynx, one of the three parts of the pharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;The area behind the voice box.&lt;SEP&gt;Lower part of the pharynx near the larynx.&lt;SEP&gt;The hypopharynx is the lower part of the throat; nasopharyngeal cancer can spread to this area.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-131</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Larynx">
      <data key="d0">Larynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific part of the throat also known as the voice box.&lt;SEP&gt;The voice box, containing vocal cords, located in the throat.&lt;SEP&gt;The voice box, examined during laryngoscopy and panendoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Voice box; tumor invasion into the larynx is a criterion for T4a stage.&lt;SEP&gt;The voice box, into which the pharynx opens.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common type of head and neck cancer, forming in the thin, flat cells of the mucus membranes of the mouth, nose, and throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that starts in the oropharynx, with squamous cell carcinoma being the most common type.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that occurs in the throat, specifically the oropharynx, and is the focus of the book.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer of the throat, particularly squamous cell carcinoma of the oropharynx, often linked to HPV.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer affecting the throat, for which p16 HPV testing is recommended.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that occurs in the throat, staged using clinical and pathologic staging, and TNM scores.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of head and neck cancer that occurs in the oropharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of head and neck cancer that is often related to HPV infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Guidelines for patients, available at NCCN.org/patientguidelines, providing information on cancer types and side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Guidelines provided by NCCN for patients, including information on fertility preservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-101</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683056</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A process that includes general health tests, fertility testing, p16 (HPV) testing, imaging tests, scoping procedures, biopsy, and biomarker testing.</data>
