    <node id="Fine-Needle Biopsy (FNB)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Biopsy (FNB)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy procedure guided by ultrasound or CT to extract tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy technique using a fine needle to remove a sample of tumor or lymph node.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy technique using a needle to remove a sample of tumor or lymph node, often guided by ultrasound or CT.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscope">
      <data key="d0">Endoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of scope often used for head and neck cancer, inserted through the mouth or nose.&lt;SEP&gt;Instruments used in panendoscopy to examine various parts of the throat and airway.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure to examine the larynx and nearby structures using a flexible scope, performed while awake with topical spray.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An exam of the back of the throat, including the larynx and hypopharynx, using a laryngoscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscope">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A thin, long tube with a fiberoptic light used in laryngoscopy to view the throat and voice box.&lt;SEP&gt;A scope used in direct laryngoscopy placed in the back of the throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Indirect Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Indirect Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A laryngoscopy method using a small mirror to view the throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A laryngoscopy method using a flexible scope passed through the nose into the throat.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure using a small flexible scope passed through the nose into the throat, typically taking less than 1 minute, with numbing medicine sprayed in the nose.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Direct Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A detailed laryngoscopy performed under general anesthesia in an operating room to see deeper and take biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A detailed exam performed under general anesthesia in an operating room using a laryngoscope to see deeper in the throat and take tissue samples for biopsy, aiming to identify the primary tumor and determine resectability.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vocal Cords">
      <data key="d0">Vocal Cords</data>
      <data key="d1">bodypart</data>
      <data key="d2">Tissues in the larynx that produce sound for speech.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-053</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Esophagoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure where a device is guided down the throat into the esophagus under anesthesia to examine the esophagus for abnormal growths.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Bronchoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure inserting a device through the nose or mouth to examine the airway, including trachea and bronchi, used to detect cancer or perform treatments, done under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Panendoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Panendoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure using different endoscopes passed down the mouth or nose to perform laryngoscopy, pharyngoscopy, esophagoscopy, or bronchoscopy, allowing thorough examination of mouth, oropharynx, larynx, esophagus, trachea, and bronchi, under anesthesia, possibly with biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy of Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy of Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy performed on a metastatic site to confirm the presence of cancer, possibly multiple sites, depending on location.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy performed on a metastatic site to confirm the presence of cancer. The type of biopsy depends on the location of the suspected metastases.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 Testing">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for programmed death-ligand 1 expression on cancer cells, measured using combined positive score (CPS), to guide treatment with immune checkpoint inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;A test to determine if cancer cells express programmed death ligand 1, which can affect immune response and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor p16 (HPV) Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor p16 (HPV) Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV testing by p16 recommended for everyone diagnosed with throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability-High (MSI-H)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability-High (MSI-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A condition where cancer cells have more than a normal number of microsatellites, often due to deficient mismatch repair genes.&lt;SEP&gt;A biomarker indicating high instability in microsatellites, often associated with response to immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-152&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1)">
      <data key="d0">Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An immune protein that, if expressed on cancer cells, can suppress the anti-tumor immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;A protein on cancer cells that binds to PD-1 on immune cells, suppressing immune response; targeted by some immune checkpoint inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-055</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Deoxyribonucleic Acid (DNA)">
      <data key="d0">Deoxyribonucleic Acid (DNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecules inside cells that are tightly packaged into chromosomes and contain genetic information.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound (US)">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound (US)</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging technique that might be used to guide a lymph node biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;A test that uses sound waves to create images of the inside of the body, used in liver cancer screening.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-160</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Protein">
      <data key="d0">Protein</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecules made by cells based on genetic instructions; some are involved in immune response and cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecules that carry out important functions in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecules that perform various functions in cells; some are receptors or markers.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecules that perform various functions; the text discusses proteins like HER2 and PD-1.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683060</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Cancer is a malignant disease characterized by the abnormal and uncontrolled growth of cells in the tissues of the cervix, the lower part of the uterus connecting to the vagina. It is a common cancer of the female reproductive system and is almost always caused by a persistent infection with the human papillomavirus (HPV). The disease often originates in the transformation zone of the cervix, where the cells are particularly susceptible to HPV-related changes. Cervical cancer shares a strong etiological link with anal cancer, as both are frequently driven by HPV; consequently, a history of cervical cancer increases the risk of developing anal cancer.

The disease presents in several histological forms, with the most common types being squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, and adenosquamous carcinoma. Clinically, cervical cancer can be classified as locally advanced or metastatic, and its progression is formally staged using the FIGO (International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics) system. The stage of the disease at diagnosis is a critical factor in determining the appropriate treatment approach, which varies according to the extent of the cancer. As a significant health concern, cervical cancer is frequently the subject of clinical discussions regarding its management, treatment options, and therapeutic considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-132&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-134&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-136&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-139&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-141&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-143&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-186&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187</data>
