    <node id="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that starts in the rectum, often adenocarcinoma. It is common and treatable, especially when found early.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that forms in the rectum, often starting from adenomatous polyps. Most rectal cancers are adenocarcinomas.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that occurs in the rectum, requiring staging and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A disease characterized by the growth of malignant cells in the rectum, often forming from polyps or lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;A disease characterized by the growth of malignant cells in the rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that starts in the rectum. NCCN Guidelines for Patients are available on this cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683063</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-cancerous growths on the inner lining of the colon and rectum. The most common type is adenoma, which can become cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;Overgrowths of cells on the inner lining of the rectal wall, which can be adenomas and may become cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-cancerous growths on the inner lining of the colon and rectum that can become cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683063</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A term for cancers that form in either the colon or rectum. Treatment differs between colon and rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that occurs in the colon or rectum, increasingly diagnosed in young adults.&lt;SEP&gt;A cancer of the colon or rectum; mentioned as related to certain germline mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer associated with MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer of the colon or rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer; personal or family history may be relevant to thyroid cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-085&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-146</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683063</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures of the lymphatic system and integral components of the body’s immune system. Distributed throughout the body, they act as filters for lymph fluid and help fight infection. In the context of cancer, lymph nodes are significant because they can be primary sites of metastasis, where cancer cells spread from a primary tumor. Regional lymph nodes are those located near the tumor, and sentinel lymph nodes are specifically the first nodes to which cancer cells are expected to drain and spread. Cancer involvement in lymph nodes can cause them to swell, and this spread is often detected through imaging techniques such as PET/CT scans, as well as through biopsies. Because they are common sites of metastasis, lymph nodes are frequently biopsied to determine whether cancer has spread, which is a critical step in staging and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-104&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-130&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-158&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Central Nervous System">
      <data key="d0">Central Nervous System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The brain and spinal cord. ALL can be found in the CNS.&lt;SEP&gt;An organ system that can be affected by ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;The part of the nervous system consisting of the brain and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;The brain and spinal cord, where glial cells support nerve cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The system comprising the brain and spinal cord, where tumors can cause problems with brain function.&lt;SEP&gt;The part of the nervous system consisting of the brain and spinal cord; the report indicates if the tumor started there.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-164&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Steroids">
      <data key="d0">Steroids</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of treatment used for ALL, often combined with multiagent chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of medication used in the treatment of ALL.</data>
