    </node>
    <node id="Large Bowel">
      <data key="d0">Large Bowel</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Another term for the large intestine, which includes the colon and rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-cancerous growths that form on the inner lining of the colon and rectum. Most rectal cancers start in polyps, and removing them can prevent cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A growth on the inner lining of the colon or rectum that may become cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;An overgrowth of cells on the inner lining of the colon wall, with types including adenoma, pedunculated, and sessile.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperplastic Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Hyperplastic Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of polyp that rarely turns into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of polyp that rarely turns into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d0">Polypectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A minor surgical procedure used to remove polyps during a colonoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;A minor surgical procedure to remove polyps during a colonoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient guideline document providing information on finding polyps early, available at NCCN.org/patientguidelines and on the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app.&lt;SEP&gt;A guideline document for patients about colorectal cancer screening.&lt;SEP&gt;A patient guideline document providing information on colorectal cancer screening.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR) Genes">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR) Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genes that fix damaged DNA; mutations in these genes can lead to rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="dMMR">
      <data key="d0">dMMR</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair deficient; an abnormal result indicating a lack of MMR function in tumor cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Deficient mismatch repair, a condition where DNA mutations prevent errors from being fixed.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-105</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MSS">
      <data key="d0">MSS</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite stable; a normal result indicating no microsatellite instability.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="pMMR">
      <data key="d0">pMMR</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair proficient; a normal result indicating proper MMR function.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare inherited cancer syndrome causing hundreds to thousands of polyps in the colon and rectum, which can become invasive cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CEA Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">CEA Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A test measuring carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) levels in the blood, used to monitor colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A test measuring carcinoembryonic antigen levels in the blood, often elevated in colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ctDNA Testing">
      <data key="d0">ctDNA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A liquid biopsy that looks for circulating tumor DNA in the blood to detect microscopic cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liquid Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Liquid Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Another name for ctDNA testing, which looks for small pieces of DNA released by tumor cells into the blood.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood test that looks for cancer cells in the blood, sometimes used as an alternative to tissue biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood test that looks for cancer cells in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-059&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-092</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Endorectal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An imaging test using sound waves via a probe inserted into the rectum to assess the extent of rectal cancer and guide biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d0">Sperm Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A fertility preservation method that stores semen by freezing in liquid nitrogen.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of preserving fertility by freezing semen in liquid nitrogen for later use, also known as semen cryopreservation.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility preservation method that stores semen by freezing it in liquid nitrogen; also called semen cryopreservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d0">Egg Freezing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A fertility preservation method that removes, freezes, and stores unfertilized eggs.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of preserving fertility by removing, freezing, and storing unfertilized eggs for later use, also called oocyte cryopreservation.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove, freeze, and store unfertilized eggs for later use.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility preservation method that removes, freezes, and stores unfertilized eggs; also called oocyte cryopreservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Tissue Banking">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Tissue Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A fertility preservation method that involves freezing ovarian tissue containing eggs.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of preserving fertility by removing part or all of an ovary and freezing the part containing eggs, which can later be unfrozen and put back in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility preservation method that removes part or all of an ovary and freezes the egg-containing tissue for later reimplantation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Transposition</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure to move ovaries out of the radiation field to preserve fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure that moves one or both ovaries out of the range of radiation beam to protect them during radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery to move ovaries out of the radiation field to preserve function.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure that moves ovaries and fallopian tubes out of the radiation field; also called oophoropexy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Deficiency">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Deficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker indicating defects in DNA mismatch repair, tested in rectal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A biomarker indicating a defect in DNA repair mechanisms, also known as dMMR.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High Microsatellite Instability">
      <data key="d0">High Microsatellite Instability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker indicating high instability in microsatellites, associated with mismatch repair deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Premature Ovarian Insufficiency">
      <data key="d0">Premature Ovarian Insufficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Damage to ovaries causing early menopause, a side effect of radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Steroid">
      <data key="d0">Steroid</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A medication that may be given before scans to lower risk of allergic reactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pacemaker">
      <data key="d0">Pacemaker</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical device implanted in the heart; people with pacemakers generally cannot have MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Technician">
      <data key="d0">Technician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A person who operates the CT scan machine and is nearby during the scan, able to communicate with the patient.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who operates imaging equipment and communicates with the patient during scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Operates the radiation machine from an adjacent room.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who operates imaging equipment and monitors patients during scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who operates the radiation machine and monitors the patient during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-187&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Semen Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Semen Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The medical term for sperm banking, which involves freezing semen for later use.&lt;SEP&gt;The medical term for sperm banking; freezing semen for later use.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oocyte Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Oocyte Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The medical term for egg freezing, which involves freezing unfertilized eggs for later use.&lt;SEP&gt;The medical term for egg freezing; freezing unfertilized eggs for later use.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oophoropexy">
      <data key="d0">Oophoropexy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The medical name for ovarian transposition, a surgical procedure to move ovaries out of radiation range.&lt;SEP&gt;The medical term for ovarian transposition, a surgery to move ovaries out of the radiation field.&lt;SEP&gt;The medical term for ovarian transposition; moving ovaries and fallopian tubes out of radiation range.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Proficient">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Proficient</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker status indicating that cancer cells have normal mismatch repair function.&lt;SEP&gt;A biomarker indicating normal mismatch repair function, also known as pMMR.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Stable">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Stable</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker status indicating that microsatellites are stable, associated with mismatch repair proficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;A status indicating that the tumor does not have defects in DNA mismatch repair, often relevant for treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A biomarker indicating no microsatellite instability, often associated with pMMR.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescent and Young Adult Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Adolescent and Young Adult Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of cancer affecting adolescents and young adults, for which NCCN guidelines provide fertility information.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-060</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adenomas">
      <data key="d0">Adenomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common type of polyps that can become invasive cancer over many years.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pedunculated Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Pedunculated Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps shaped like mushrooms with a stalk and round top, sticking out from the colon wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps shaped like mushrooms that stick out from the colon wall, with a stalk and round top.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sessile Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Sessile Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Flatter polyps without a stalk, more likely to return after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps that are flatter in shape.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A polyp in which cancer has just started to grow.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Resection">
      <data key="d0">Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical removal of a larger piece of the rectum when needed after polypectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery to remove a bigger piece of the colon when needed after polypectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical removal of cancer growths, sometimes used for metastatic disease.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure that attempts to remove the entire tumor or as much as safely possible, serving as both a biopsy and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical removal of bladder tissue for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-166&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging technique used to guide decisions about surgery for sessile polyps and for surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal cancer that has not spread far and may be treated with local excision or surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T1 Tumor">
      <data key="d0">T1 Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor that hasn't grown beyond the second (muscle) layer of the rectal wall.&lt;SEP&gt;A smaller tumor that has not invaded the muscle layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A minimally invasive procedure to remove small rectal cancers in one piece, often curing them.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Mucosal Resection">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Mucosal Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Another name for endoscopic submucosal dissection.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flexible Sigmoidoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Flexible Sigmoidoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure used for surveillance after ESD to check for recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical removal of T1 tumors located at the end of the rectum through the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced technique for removing tumors higher in the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced technique for removing tumors higher in the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Proctoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Proctoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure used for surveillance after transanal local excision.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to see inside the anus and rectum using a proctoscope.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to look for signs of cancer in the bowel, often performed under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-137</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transabdominal Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Preferred next treatment when more treatment is needed after local excision.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical approach involving an incision in the abdomen to remove rectal tumors and nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T2 Tumor">
      <data key="d0">T2 Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor that has invaded the muscle layer of the rectal wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dr. Google">
      <data key="d0">Dr. Google</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A colloquial term for searching symptoms online, discouraged in favor of reputable sources.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-061</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure to remove a tumor and some surrounding tissue, often used for early-stage rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure to remove only the tumor and a small margin of surrounding tissue, used for some early-stage perianal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure to remove cancer, considered only for low-grade (G1) and intermediate-grade (G2) perianal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical option for some slow-growing stage I and II perianal cancers, instead of chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-190&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Low Anterior Resection (LAR)">
      <data key="d0">Low Anterior Resection (LAR)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of transabdominal surgery for tumors in the mid to upper rectum, removing part of the colon and reconnecting if possible.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of transabdominal surgery for lower rectal tumors, involving removal of the anus and rectum and creation of a permanent colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A radical surgery to remove the anus, used for recurrent or progressive anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure for anal cancer that removes the anus, rectum, and part of the colon, often resulting in a colostomy. The text includes questions about this surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-191&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-197</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colostomy">
      <data key="d0">Colostomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure creating an opening (stoma) on the abdomen for stool to exit the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure connecting the colon to an opening on the abdomen (stoma) to divert stool flow.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure that connects the colon to the abdominal wall to divert stool through a stoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure that connects the colon to the outside of the abdomen, creating a stoma for stool to exit the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical opening created during APR surgery for stool to leave the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical opening in the abdomen for waste elimination, often resulting from APR. The text asks who can help with colostomy care.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-194&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-197</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T3 Cancer">
      <data key="d0">T3 Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A larger cancer that has not spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Anastomosis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical reconnection of the colon to the remaining rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Coloanal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d0">Coloanal Anastomosis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical reconnection of the colon directly to the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stoma">
      <data key="d0">Stoma</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An opening on the abdomen created during a colostomy for waste elimination.&lt;SEP&gt;An opening on the surface of the abdomen created during a colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical opening on the abdomen through which urine or stool exits the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgically created opening in the abdominal wall for urine to exit the body in certain urinary diversions.&lt;SEP&gt;An opening in the abdomen created during urinary diversion surgery, through which urine is drained.&lt;SEP&gt;An opening on the surface of the abdomen created by a colostomy, through which stool exits into a bag.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-144&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-182&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-192</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that has grown into the second or third layer of the rectal wall but not spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that has grown through the rectal wall and possibly into nearby tissues or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3 Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3 Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Irinotecan">
      <data key="d0">Irinotecan</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemotherapy drug that can cause abdominal cramping, nausea, diarrhea, and hair loss.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemotherapy drug that tends to cause abdominal cramping, nausea, diarrhea, and hair loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A disease characterized by malignant cells in the kidneys.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that starts in the tissues of the kidneys, with most being carcinomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that arises in the kidney, with renal cell carcinoma being the most common type.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RCC Subtypes">
      <data key="d0">RCC Subtypes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Different subtypes of renal cell carcinoma, a type of kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sigmoid Colon">
      <data key="d0">Sigmoid Colon</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The S-shaped section of the colon that connects to the rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the four main sections of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Levator Ani">
      <data key="d0">Levator Ani</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A muscle of the pelvic floor that supports the rectum and may be involved in lower rectal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neuropathy">
      <data key="d0">Neuropathy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nerve damage that can be caused by oxaliplatin, leading to numbness, tingling, or pain in extremities.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Capsule">
      <data key="d0">Renal Capsule</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A thin layer of tissue covering the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary System">
      <data key="d0">Urinary System</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of organs that remove waste from the body in the form of urine, including the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ureters">
      <data key="d0">Ureters</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder. Part of the urothelial lining.&lt;SEP&gt;Tubes that carry urine from kidneys to bladder, examined in ureteroscopy and urogram.&lt;SEP&gt;Tubes that carry urine from kidneys to bladder; connected to the conduit or neobladder in urinary diversions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683065</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Colon cancer is a disease characterized by the growth of malignant cells in the colon, a part of the large intestine. It is a distinct form of cancer with treatment approaches that differ from those used for rectal cancer. The disease commonly originates from polyps, particularly adenomatous polyps, which are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon that can become cancerous over time. Colon cancer is considered a common and treatable form of cancer, especially when detected early, and it can present in various stages, including stages 1, 2, and 3, as well as in non-metastatic or metastatic forms.

The condition may be linked to family history, suggesting a potential genetic or hereditary component that can increase an individual's risk. Additionally, research indicates that having colon cancer may be associated with an elevated risk of developing prostate cancer. As a disease, colon cancer involves the uncontrolled division of abnormal cells within the colon, and its progression and severity are typically classified by staging systems that help guide prognosis and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-121&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-122&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-123&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-124&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-125&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-126</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Gland">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A gland that sits on top of each kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gerota's Fascia">
      <data key="d0">Gerota's Fascia</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">An outer layer of fibrous tissue covering the fat around the kidney and adrenal gland.&lt;SEP&gt;A layer of connective tissue surrounding the kidney, which can be invaded by locally advanced kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Artery">
      <data key="d0">Renal Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The blood vessel that carries blood with waste into the kidney.&lt;SEP&gt;The blood vessel through which blood flows into the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Tubules">
      <data key="d0">Renal Tubules</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Tiny filtering tubes in the kidney that remove waste and other substances from the blood to make urine.&lt;SEP&gt;Tiny tubes in the kidneys that remove waste from blood and make urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Vein">
      <data key="d0">Renal Vein</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The blood vessel that carries clean, filtered blood out of the kidney.&lt;SEP&gt;The blood vessel through which blood flows out of the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vena Cava">
      <data key="d0">Vena Cava</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A large vein that takes clean blood from the renal vein back to the heart.&lt;SEP&gt;A large vein that receives blood from the renal vein and can be invaded by kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">Renal Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A hollow space in the middle of the kidney where urine collects before leaving through the ureter.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common type of kidney cancer, starting in the cells lining the renal tubules, accounting for about 9 out of 10 kidney cancers. It can be broken down into subtypes based on histology.&lt;SEP&gt;The most common type of kidney cancer, starting in cells lining the renal tubules.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A less common cancer that starts in the cells lining the renal pelvis and ureter, sharing features with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wilms Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Wilms Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of kidney cancer that usually occurs in young children, also known as nephroblastoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare kidney cancer that starts in the blood vessels or connective tissue of the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma, occurring in about 7 out of 10 people with RCC, with cells that look pale or clear under a microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;A subtype of renal cell carcinoma characterized by clear cells under a microscope, occurring in about 7 out of 10 people with RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A subtype of renal cell carcinoma that is not clear cell, also called nccRCC, with several subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;A subtype of renal cell carcinoma that includes several subtypes, such as papillary RCC, chromophobe RCC, translocation RCC, and unclassified RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient guideline document for bladder cancer, available at NCCN.org/patientguidelines and on the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Soft Tissue Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Soft Tissue Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient guideline document for soft tissue sarcoma, available at NCCN.org/patientguidelines and on the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transitional Cell Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Transitional Cell Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An older term for urothelial carcinoma, which starts in the cells lining the renal pelvis and ureter.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary RCC">
      <data key="d0">Papillary RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common subtype of non-clear cell RCC, occurring in about 1 out of 10 people with RCC, characterized by finger-like growths under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromophobe RCC">
      <data key="d0">Chromophobe RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A subtype of non-clear cell RCC with pale cells that are larger than clear cells and look different.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d0">Translocation RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A subtype of RCC that can be found at all ages but tends to be diagnosed in children or younger adults, with unique gene fusions that promote cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Unclassified RCC">
      <data key="d0">Unclassified RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A subtype of RCC where cancer cells don't look like any of the other subtypes or more than one subtype is present.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Collecting Duct RCC">
      <data key="d0">Collecting Duct RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare type of non-clear cell RCC that forms in the cells of the collecting ducts or tubules.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Medullary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Medullary Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare type of non-clear cell RCC that can be found in young people of African descent who carry sickle cell trait or disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A recently defined type of RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC">
      <data key="d0">Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A recently defined type of RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ELOC-Mutated RCC">
      <data key="d0">ELOC-Mutated RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A recently defined type of RCC, formerly known as TCEB1-mutated RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid RCC">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of renal cell carcinoma where cancer cells look like sarcoma cells, occurring in less than a certain percentage of RCC tumors, tending to grow quickly and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Urine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests that analyze urine samples to detect abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Caregiver">
      <data key="d0">Caregiver</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A person who provides care and support to the patient, may ask questions and take notes during appointments.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who provides support to a patient, such as transportation, meals, or emotional support.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sickle Cell Trait">
      <data key="d0">Sickle Cell Trait</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic condition where a person carries one sickle cell gene, associated with renal medullary carcinoma in young people of African descent.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sickle Cell Disease">
      <data key="d0">Sickle Cell Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic blood disorder that can cause sickling of red blood cells, associated with renal medullary carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bones">
      <data key="d0">Bones</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Skeletal structures, a common site for distant metastasis of kidney cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Common site for prostate cancer metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The skeletal structures of the body; a site where prostate cancer can metastasize.&lt;SEP&gt;Skeletal structures that are potential sites of distant metastasis for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A common site for metastatic bladder cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic bones may be checked for cancer spread via MRI.&lt;SEP&gt;Structures that may be affected by cancer spread, checked via bone scan.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-081&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-083&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-142&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer of connective tissues such as muscles, nerves, fat, blood vessels, and fibrous tissue, which sarcomatoid RCC cells resemble.&lt;SEP&gt;A rare group of cancers that includes soft tissue sarcomas and bone sarcomas.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that includes bone cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A rare type of cancer that starts in blood vessel cells of the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-064&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-153&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-157&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159</data>
