    <node id="Infections">
      <data key="d0">Infections</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Diseases caused by pathogens that white blood cells fight.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urethra">
      <data key="d0">Urethra</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The urethra is a tube that carries urine from the bladder out of the body, serving as the final passageway for urine expulsion. In males, the prostate gland wraps around the urethra just beneath the bladder, and an enlarged prostate can compress the tube, affecting urine flow. The urethra is part of the urothelial lining, which is the tissue layer that lines parts of the urinary tract.

Clinically, the urethra is examined during cystoscopy, a procedure used to view the inside of the bladder and urethra, and it also serves as the access pathway for cystoscopy and for transurethral resection of bladder tumors (TURBT). In cases of radical cystectomy—surgical removal of the bladder, often due to cancer—the urethra may also be removed if the cancer has spread into it. Additionally, the urethra can be invaded by advanced anal tumors, particularly in stage IIIB disease, indicating its vulnerability to local cancer spread from adjacent structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-063&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-079&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-080&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-087&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-135&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-173&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-174&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-176&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-177&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-180&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-181&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-188</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotype">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotype</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The classification of leukemia cells based on the type of lymphocyte and maturity.&lt;SEP&gt;The pattern of antigen expression on cells, used to classify leukemias.&lt;SEP&gt;The pattern of surface proteins on cells, used to identify the correct type of cancer, such as CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;The identifiable pattern of proteins on the surface of cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-066&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-169&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-070</data>
