    <node id="Lymph Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph Vessels are a component of the lymphatic system, functioning as thin-walled tubes that transport lymph fluid—a clear liquid containing lymphocytes and other immune cells—throughout the body. These vessels are integral to the circulatory and immune systems, moving lymph from peripheral tissues toward lymph nodes, where immune responses are initiated. They are present in various organs and tissues, including the breast, where they drain lymph fluid from breast tissue to regional lymph nodes, and the liver, where they form part of the organ's lymphatic drainage network. Additionally, lymph vessels are located in the submucosa of certain organs, facilitating the movement of lymph and serving as a conduit for immune surveillance.

Beyond their physiological role in fluid balance and immunity, lymph vessels are clinically significant in the context of cancer. Malignant cells can invade these vessels and travel through them to distant sites, a primary mechanism of metastatic spread. In the breast, for example, cancer cells may enter lymph vessels and be carried to axillary lymph nodes, influencing staging and treatment decisions. Furthermore, when lymph vessels are blocked—such as by tumor emboli or metastatic deposits—lymph fluid can accumulate in surrounding tissues, leading to localized swelling, a condition known as lymphedema. This obstruction can occur in various regions, including the breast and liver, and represents a common complication of advanced cancer or surgical disruption of lymphatic pathways. Thus, lymph vessels serve both as essential conduits for immune transport and as potential routes for disease dissemination, with their blockage posing significant clinical challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-065&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-076&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-095&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-096&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-097&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-098&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-100&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-159&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-162</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683073</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
