      <data key="d5">1788683080</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Amenorrhea">
      <data key="d0">Amenorrhea</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The cessation of menstrual periods that may occur during treatment and for up to 2 years after, often returning in those 35 and under.&lt;SEP&gt;The absence of menstrual periods, which may occur during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-111&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683080</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology Report">
      <data key="d0">Pathology Report</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pathology Report is a formal medical document prepared by a pathologist, and in specific cases, a neuropathologist, following the laboratory analysis of biopsy samples or tissue specimens. The report details the findings from the microscopic examination of the biopsied lesions, explicitly recording the type and features of the tissue. Its primary purpose is to communicate the presence and extent of cancer, providing a definitive diagnosis based on the histological analysis.

The content of the report includes critical diagnostic information such as the tumor's histology, grade, and stage, as well as specific biomarkers when applicable. These results are essential for the patient's care team to understand the nature of the disease and to plan appropriate next steps. Consequently, the Pathology Report serves as a cornerstone for clinical decision-making, providing the lab results necessary for establishing a prognosis and formulating an effective treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-091&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-108&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-167&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-170&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-178</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683082</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cerebrospinal Fluid">
      <data key="d0">Cerebrospinal Fluid</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A liquid that surrounds the brain and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;A liquid that flows in and around the brain and spinal cord, protecting and nourishing the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluid that flows in and around the brain, which can be blocked by a tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-165</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683082</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Eyes">
      <data key="d0">Eyes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Organs of sight that are connected directly to the brain and part of the CNS.&lt;SEP&gt;Organs connected to the brain via optic nerves, where primary CNS lymphoma can spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-112&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-115</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788683082</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
