    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d7">12.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The relation (Biopsy, Pathologist) describes the professional interaction and workflow between a biopsy procedure and a pathologist. A biopsy involves the removal of a sample of tissue, such as tumor tissue, from a patient. This sample is subsequently sent to a pathologist, who is a medical specialist responsible for its examination. The pathologist analyzes the cells, tissues, and organs obtained during the biopsy, typically using a microscope, to look for the presence of cancer cells.

The primary purpose of this examination is to confirm whether cancer is present and to reliably diagnose the specific disease. Beyond general cancer detection, the pathologist may also perform specialized assessments, such as assigning Gleason patterns, which are used to grade certain types of cancer. The pathologist’s role is central to the diagnostic process, as their analysis of the biopsy sample provides the definitive information needed to confirm a cancer diagnosis and guide subsequent medical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d9">analysis,analyzed by,analyzes,diagnosis,diagnostic procedure,examination,examines,performed by,tests,tissue analysis</data>
      <data key="d10">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-054&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-084&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-086&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-099&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-103&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-117&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-129&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-147</data>
      <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
